docs/production-readiness-deep-scan-2026-05-28.md:504:> **Status: ✅ Resolved** in PR #308 (2026-06-13) — took **Route A (keep + RLS)**, NOT the scan's "drop `google_connections`". Migration **066** enables deny-all RLS (enable RLS, no policy) on all three; the server reaches them only via the RLS-bypassing Postgres.js `postgres` role (mirrors `analytics_events`/`flagged_responses`/`impersonation_sessions`). All three added to `TENANT_TABLES` (→ `REQUIRED_RLS_TABLES`) so `ci:rls-audit` now guards them, plus matching `docs/security/rls-inventory.md` rows and **17** `tenant-scope-allowlist.ts` structural exemptions (baseline 340→357; `google_connections` needs none — all access is via identifier-interpolating purge helpers). **`google_connections` was NOT dropped — the scan's "dead table" claim is stale:** owner-offboarding now references it as a `hard_delete` purge target in 4 sites (`table-disposition`/`purge-stages`/`purge-helpers`/`purge-verification`), so a drop would require ripping those out. Verified **no anon/browser table access** (all via the service `sql` client), so deny-all is transparent to the public demo viewer + every ingress/admin/purge path. Migration is **hand-applied** (single transaction), staging-then-prod, both Supabase projects.
docs/production-readiness-deep-scan-2026-05-28.md:549:- **Evidence:** `backoff: { type: 'exponential', delay: 5_000 }` — pure exponential, **no jitter** → a provider outage hitting many jobs retries in lockstep (5s/10s/20s), a thundering-herd against the recovering dependency. Separately, `defaultJobOptions` sets no `removeOnComplete`/`removeOnFail`/`keepJobs` → BullMQ retains **every** finished job record in Redis forever. On Upstash (per-command + per-storage billing) this is unbounded steady-state growth — the Postgres analog (`ArchivalPoller`) exists, but there's no equivalent for Redis job records.
docs/production-readiness-deep-scan-2026-05-28.md:610:> **Status: ✅ Resolved** in PR #313 (2026-06-13) — Option A (non-terminal retry): on no-account-by-`stripe_customer_id`, `subscription-updated.ts`/`subscription-deleted.ts` now THROW `AccountNotProvisionedYetError` instead of returning cleanly, so the ingress wrapper marks the event `failed` + replies **500** and Stripe redelivers once `checkout-completed` has written the customer id. Redelivery is idempotent (status-guarded UPDATE + `claimInboundEvent` failed-row reclaim + `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`). The subscription-**mismatch** / offboarding-snapshot branches stay clean returns (real mismatches, not timing). Out-of-order sequence + exactly-once proven by new tests in `stripe-webhook-handlers.test.ts`. A genuinely orphaned customer retries until Stripe's ~3-day schedule gives up; the hourly reconcile poller remains the standing backstop. (Option B — pre-create the Stripe customer at session creation — deliberately NOT taken; Option A + reconcile is sufficient for launch.) Worker-touching → staging-verified before merge.
docs/production-readiness-deep-scan-2026-05-28.md:715:> **Status: ⏸️ Deferred (deliberate)** (2026-06-14) — **erasure (Art.17) is shipped; data EXPORT (Art.15 access/portability) is deliberately deferred until the first EU/UK paying customer** — the scan's own trigger — exactly like §3.4 and §7.4. The owner-offboarding cascade (spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-05-owner-offboarding-design.md`, migrations 056–061) gives a tested **erasure** path (`cancel_only` / `delete_data`) driven by the single-source-of-truth table map `src/lib/account-offboarding/table-disposition.ts` (33 tenant tables, each with disposition + scope evidence + purge-verification mode), with admin (`/api/admin/offboardings/*`) and owner (`/api/account/offboarding/*`) routes and an in-tx `account_audit_log` / `account_offboardings` record. **No Art.15 export path exists** — the offboarding `mode` schema accepts only `cancel_only` / `delete_data` (an `export_data` mode is rejected `400` by design; `src/app/api/account/offboarding/__tests__/confirm.test.ts` pins this), and `src/lib/legal/content.ts` states RepWorth provides no self-serve export. **Interim (pre-trigger) fulfillment:** an admin can satisfy a manual Art.15 request using that same `table-disposition.ts` map + one-off `account_id`/`business_id`-scoped reads — the capability exists, only the self-serve endpoint is deferred. **When built** (admin-operated minimum first, per the recommendation): an outbox-enqueued (invariant #2) Railway worker job (invariant #1) that DERIVES its table list from `table-disposition.ts` so export and purge can never drift — including the `retain_deidentified` tables (`accounts`/`businesses`/`users`) as the subject's own data — streams paginated JSON (no in-memory batch), writes an audit entry, and passes `ci:tenant-scope` + `ci:rls-audit`. Like §3.4 / §7.4, a conscious deferral with a documented trigger, not an oversight.
