.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:45:| `solicitation_batches` | No time-based expiry deletion in watch-mode; hard-delete with the source upload on account purge | account-offboarding purge via `contact_csv_uploads` cascade |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:46:| `solicitation_asks` | No time-based expiry deletion in watch-mode; authoritative decisions outlive 180-day run archival and hard-delete with their batch on account purge | `solicitation_batches` cascade |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:47:| `solicitation_consent` | **No TTL — consent rows never expire (§7.3)**; a consent row outliving or predeceasing its contact would desynchronize the two. Hard-deleted with the contact on per-contact delete (same transaction, by handle — the ledger has no FK to `solicitation_contacts`) and on offboarding purge (`must_be_absent`) — consent fails closed; suppression fails open (DR1), which is why these rows are destroyed where #621's suppression rows are retained | contact-delete path (#625) + `businesses` `ON DELETE CASCADE`; purge verification via `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` (`hard_delete`, `direct_business_id`) |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:48:| `solicitation_outreach` | **Row kept; body redacted.** `rendered_subject`/`rendered_body` NULLed and `interpolation_inputs` emptied to `'{}'`, with `body_redacted_at` stamped, 90 days after the body existed — the send *fact* (status, disposition, provider id, bounce/complaint signals) is retained indefinitely for suppression, over-contact history and the daily cap, while the *content* ages out. **One window, two clocks (092):** a `sent` row ages from `sent_at`; a row that went terminal WITHOUT sending (`withheld`/`cancelled`/`failed`/`unknown`) has no `sent_at` and ages from `rendered_at`. A live row (`rendered`/`queued`/`sending`) is never redactable at any age — a bodyless live attempt can neither send nor settle. Hard-deletes with its batch on account purge | ArchivalPoller arms 9 + 10 (migrations 091/092, indexes `idx_solicitation_outreach_redaction_sweep` and `idx_solicitation_outreach_unsent_terminal_redaction_sweep`); purge via `solicitation_batches` cascade |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:49:| `solicitation_suppressions` | **No TTL — an opt-out never expires (§2.1)**; a time limit on an opt-out is a violation with a cron job attached. Splits on **Suppression scope** at offboarding purge (D7): `scope='global'` rows are **retained** with `account_id` intact — the recipient's revocation against our one shared sending identity is not the account's data, and deleting one would let us mail a person who opted out (#535 §6 through a different door) — while `scope='account'` (`owner_manual`) rows hard-delete with the account. The only removal path for a retained row is the admin-only, audited **Correction path** (#645) | ArchivalPoller deliberately untouched; offboarding purge content stage + `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` (`retain_compliance_limited_pii`, `compliance_handle_hash`, `allowed_limited_retention` — ONE entry for both scopes, #643 G2) |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:50:| `solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` | **No TTL, no expiry** (§2.2): CAN-SPAM §7704(a)(3) requires the link operate ≥30 days, and there is no expiry value better than none. **Row kept; provenance scrubbed.** The offboarding purge NULLs `account_id`/`business_id`/`ask_id` and keeps `token_hash`, `normalized_handle`, `channel` and `sent_at`, so redemption still works after the sender is gone (§5.2). `outreach_id` nulls independently via `ON DELETE SET NULL` (#643 G1) whenever its outreach row is deleted, account alive or not | ArchivalPoller deliberately untouched; offboarding purge token scrub + `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` (`retain_compliance_limited_pii`, `compliance_handle_hash`, `allowed_limited_retention`) |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:51:| `solicitation_suppression_corrections` | **No TTL.** The record proves an opt-out was removed and by whom; deleting it would erase the only evidence that the removal was audited. Retains `normalized_handle` indefinitely — the minimum needed to identify WHICH opt-out was removed, since `suppression_id` points at a deleted row — matching the retention posture of the `scope='global'` ledger rows it describes (spec D7). **Row kept WHOLE**, including the corrected row's `account_id`, `created_by_user_id` and `note` (D-A11): those are the evidence the removed assertion was legitimate, and for a `scope='account'` row `account_id` is the **Suppression scope** key rather than provenance | No poller touches it; the offboarding purge neither deletes nor scrubs it. Classified in `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` (`retain_compliance_limited_pii`, `compliance_handle_hash`, `allowed_limited_retention`), and its purge-verification arm inverts the siblings above — it counts a record that survived having LOST its evidence, not one that survived at all |
.claude/reference/migrations-log.md:52:| `solicitation_send_breaker` | **No TTL** — a **Send breaker trip** and its reset are the audit record for a platform-wide sending halt, and this table is the only sink that can hold one (`account_audit_log` is account-scoped, this event is not). Append-only: a trip row is never updated except by the single admin reset that closes it, and never deleted. **Not in `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS`**: the table carries no `account_id` and no customer data, so account offboarding has nothing to purge and purge verification has nothing to assert | No poller touches it; the offboarding purge neither deletes nor scrubs it. Not a `TENANT_TABLES` member (no `account_id`) — deny-all RLS instead, migration 110 |
