.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1657:FAIL → the P2 hardening installed a 10s `AbortSignal.timeout` fetch as the GLOBAL fetch of the shared `supabaseServer` client, to stop worker-side admin/storage calls from hanging BullMQ slots. But supabase-js `global.fetch` applies to EVERY call on that client — including Auth identity mutations (signup provisioning, invites). WHY → a mutation that commits remotely but responds >10s surfaces client-side as a TimeoutError with no reconciliation path: the Auth identity exists, the app never learns it, and the user later hits `account_exists` lockouts. A timeout on a READ is safe (retry); a timeout on a NON-IDEMPOTENT identity mutation is an ambiguous-outcome hazard. CORRECT → split by call class, not one global bound: `supabaseServer` stays UNBOUNDED for identity-mutating flows (all Next.js signup/invite paths), and the separate `supabaseServerBounded` (`boundedSupabaseFetch`, `SUPABASE_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=10_000`, `src/lib/db/server.ts`) serves worker-side admin/storage calls where hanging is worse than ambiguity (offboarding purge, `CleanupAbandonedSignupsPoller` — whose `Promise.race` belt stays, since it also covers non-fetch hangs). Tests pin which client gets the injected fetch. General rule: a client-wide timeout is a per-endpoint-semantics decision in disguise — never bound idempotent reads and ambiguous mutations with one knob.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1702:### [2026-07-26] The `csv_ingest` advisory-lock contract is a STATUS-TRANSITION contract, not a table contract — the offboarding purge deletes unlocked, so a 0-row terminal write is tolerated on purpose (#582)
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1703:FAIL → an early draft of #565's plan read `src/lib/solicitation/ingest.ts`'s documented per-business concurrency contract ("every transaction that writes `contact_csv_uploads` takes `pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtext('csv_ingest_' || businessId))` as its FIRST statement") as universal, concluded that the terminal `status='pending'`-guarded UPDATE matching 0 rows was an impossible state, and specified an assertion + throw. Adversarial review caught it before implementation. WHY → there is a **fourth writer** outside the protocol: `worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:256` hard-DELETEs `contact_csv_uploads` by `account_id` with no advisory lock. Only three writers honour it (the ingest transaction, `terminalizeUploadForInactiveSubscription`, and the csv-ingest failed-hook — all now routed through the single owners `acquireCsvIngestLock` / `writeTerminalUploadState`). So the purge can commit between the ingest transaction's `status='pending'` read and its terminal write: the row vanishes mid-ingest, the terminal UPDATE matches 0 rows, and the job completes normally reporting success for an upload whose row no longer exists. That outcome is **benign** — the account was legitimately being purged. The assertion would have converted it into throw → BullMQ retry → retry exhaustion → failed-hook terminalisation → Sentry noise, i.e. an alert storm manufactured out of a correct-by-intent deletion. CORRECT → `writeTerminalUploadState` (`ingest.ts:410`) returns `void`, no caller inspects a row count, and its doc comment names `purge-stages.ts` and forbids adding the assert. Read the lock contract as scoped to **status transitions**, with the purge an explicit permanent exception — the two are not the same claim, and the narrower one is what actually holds. Enforcement today is a comment, which is the weakest possible mechanism for a contract that reads as universal from inside `ingest.ts`; #582 tracks closing it properly (take the lock in the purge stage, per-business under the account). General rule: before asserting "this state is impossible", enumerate every writer of the table, not every writer that follows the protocol.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1714:FAIL → `scripts/verify-gate.sh --review` failed intermittently (≈50%) with `PostgresError: deadlock detected` (40P01), always in a live-DB suite, never on an assertion; the victim varied between runs (`batch-builder.integration.test.ts`, `account-offboarding/integration-lifecycle.test.ts`) and even between tests within one suite, and the whole thing was invisible in CI (no `.env.local` → the suites skip) and unreproducible in isolation. WHY → the Postgres server log names both sides: one backend waiting for `RowShareLock` on `agent_runs` (`appendRunStep`'s `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`), the other waiting for `AccessExclusiveLock` on `solicitation_asks` while running **`088_solicitation_batch_executor.sql`** — a *test suite re-applying a migration in `beforeAll`*. DDL vs row locks on the same relations in opposite orders. Eight suites re-applied migration SQL to ensure their schema; `vitest.config.ts` already serializes `*.integration.test.ts` into its own project (`fileParallelism: false` + `groupOrder`), which makes this safe **within one vitest process** — but nothing in vitest spans processes, and `verify-gate.sh:70` launches every intake `verification_commands` entry in parallel with the main `test` row. #565's intake declared two of them (`pnpm test …ingest.integration.test.ts`, `…batch-builder.integration.test.ts`), so a `--review` run put three vitest processes on one database. Two more compounding facts: (1) `integration-lifecycle.test.ts` self-hydrated `.env.local`, so it ran even in a "no-export" gate run — which is why the issue's "every self-hydrating suite holds the shared lock, so this is ruled out" reasoning was wrong; and (2) its filename does not match `**/__tests__/**/*.integration.test.ts`, so it sat in the *parallel* project with ~740 other files. Differential proof: same two suites in ONE process 0/4 deadlocks, in TWO processes 3/6; the gate-shaped three-process arm 5/6 → 0/8 after the fix. CORRECT → remove the ingredient a test controls: the schema is provisioned once by `pnpm test:db:setup` and suites **assert** it (`assertSchemaReady`, which also owns the single migration→objects map) instead of applying it. A live-DB suite must also be named `*.integration.test.ts` so the serialized project actually claims it. Migration-**shape** suites legitimately apply DDL and are allowlisted, but must hold `acquireLiveDbLock` — a Postgres SESSION advisory lock is the only mechanism that serializes across processes (renamed from `feature-543-database-lock`, which had quietly become the shared protocol). Both rules are CI-enforced by `ci:live-db-suites`. General rule: "vitest runs these serially" is a claim about one process; the moment a script invokes vitest twice concurrently, every in-process guarantee is void — reach for a DB-side lock, or delete the contended operation.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1728:FAIL → #625's intake estimated ~1200 diff lines; actual ~2500, with three mid-run surface amendments. WHY → the plan priced the schema + writer but not the registry ripples a new table/event drags in: `scripts/ci/tenant-tables.ts` (`ci:rls-audit`/`ci:tenant-scope`), `docs/security/rls-inventory.md`, `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` + purge sweep (+ the spec §7.2 cascade assumption being wrong), the event_name-CHECK migration (note above) + `docs/analytics/events.md`, migrations-log retention row, `schema-ready.ts`. CORRECT → when an intake adds a table or event, enumerate the registries up front and put every one in `allowed_edit_surface`; also expect one fallow finding class only at PR time — `verify-gate.sh` excludes `ci:fallow*` BY DESIGN (own workflow/baseline), so changed-code complexity trips surface at CI, and the house fix is extracting the new logic into a small decision helper, never a `.fallowrc` waiver (waivers are for pre-existing complexity swept in mechanically).
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1745:FAIL → two #643 review findings with the same shape. (1) `token_hash TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE` carried the comment *"SHA-256, node:crypto (invariant #10); raw token NEVER stored (invariant #5)"* while accepting a raw token as readily as a digest — a single call site that forgot to hash would have stored plaintext and every reader downstream would still have worked. (2) `suppressionMatchSql` hardcoded `channel = 'email'` while `recordSuppression` took `channel` as a parameter — two owners for one dimension, failing **open**: the day `SUPPRESSION_CHANNELS` gains a member the writer mints rows the reader can never see, and an opt-out the ledger holds but the gate cannot find means the recipient gets contacted. CORRECT → (1) a named format CHECK mirroring migration 056's `account_offboarding_tokens`, `CHECK (token_hash ~ '^[a-f0-9]{64}$')`, lowercase-only because that is what `node:crypto`'s `digest('hex')` emits and admitting uppercase would let two spellings of one digest past the UNIQUE index; verified by **mutation** (remove the CHECK, re-provision, confirm 6 of 7 new assertions fail, restore). (2) `channel` became a required parameter on both sides. Bonus finding from the same review: the offboarding token scrub's `OR business_id IN (SELECT … FROM businesses …)` made `idx_solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens_account` unusable (seq scan) on the one statement that index was added for, **and** was redundant under the G12 CHECK (`business_id NOT NULL ⟹ account_id NOT NULL`). Dropping it from the scrub while KEEPING it in the purge verifier restored index usability on the hot path and preserved the cross-account tripwire on the cold one — and because the remaining predicate is natively account-scoped, it also deleted two `tenant-scope-allowlist` entries (`ALLOWLIST_BASELINE` 413 → 411). General rule: an allowlist entry that exists only because the scanner rejects `OR` is a signal worth re-reading — the `OR` may not be earning its keep.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1753:- Run spine (2): `agent_runs.error` + `agent_run_steps.detail` in `src/lib/agents/read.ts`.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1755:- One each (5): `analytics_events.properties` in `worker/background/pollers/AnalyticsDispatchPoller.ts`; `review_exceptions.payload` in `worker/jobs/AdminExceptionWorker.ts`; `account_offboardings.provider_dispositions` through the shared parser in `src/lib/account-offboarding/provider-disposition.ts`; `contact_csv_uploads.report` through the shared parser `parseIngestReportValue` in `src/lib/solicitation/ingest.ts` (consumed by `getUploadStatus` in `uploads.ts` and the worker's terminal-status re-read); `solicitation_asks.evidence` through `parseEvidence` in `src/lib/solicitation/batch-view.ts` (the Requests read model — a non-object or discriminant-less value throws rather than rendering a blank spoilage row).
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1797:FAIL → a new live-DB suite shipped with no `assertSchemaReady` call, on the plan's stated belief that "`describeWithDb` handles the schema assertion". It does not — `src/__tests__/helpers/describe-with-db.ts` only gates on `TEST_DATABASE_POOL_URL` (plus the `REPWORTH_DISABLE_LIVE_DB_SUITES` kill switch) and registers `describe` or `describe.skip`. Nothing in it touches the schema. The cited precedent (`read.integration.test.ts`) also omits the assertion, so copying the nearest neighbour propagated the gap; 32 other suites do call it. WHY → CLAUDE.md says live-DB suites "**assert** their schema … so a behind-head DB fails loudly", which is a statement about the *convention*, not about the helper. Then the first fix was **still wrong**: it asserted `MIGRATION_071_RUN_SPINE` — the run-spine tables the suite READS — and missed that the code under test WRITES `agent_runs.parent_run_id`, a column added by migration **088** and declared by `MIGRATION_088_SOLICITATION_BATCH_EXECUTOR`. `MIGRATION_071_RUN_SPINE` lists only `tables`, no `columns`. A DB at head 071–087 would pass the assertion and then fail at the first `it` with the raw `column "parent_run_id" does not exist` — precisely the failure mode the assertion exists to prevent. CORRECT → call `assertSchemaReady` explicitly in every live-DB suite, and build the requirement list from **everything the transaction under test touches**, not from the tables the assertions read. Trace the writers (`startOrGetActiveRun` → `recordStep` → `completeRun`), not just the `SELECT`s. `run-spine.integration.test.ts` gets this right and asserts 071 + 088 + 091 together.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1803:FAIL → `docs/directory.md`'s `supabase/migrations/` prose paragraph still claimed migration 098 carries "deliberately **no `account_id`**, so it is correctly absent from the offboarding inventory". Both halves were false as shipped: 098 declares `account_id UUID`, and the table is `table-disposition.ts` entry `retain_compliance_limited_pii` with a `purge-verification.ts` branch. WHY → deviation D-A11 reversed that design mid-branch, and the resync commit swept "`CONTEXT.md`'s Correction path entry and two `docs/directory.md` rows" — the rows the author remembered editing. The stale claim lived ~1000 lines away in a section about migration *numbering*, which is not where anyone looks for a statement about column design. It survived two review rounds and only surfaced while hand-resolving a merge conflict in that same file. CORRECT → when a deviation reverses a design decision, `rg` the **fact** (`account_id`, "offboarding inventory") across `docs/` and `.claude/`, not the file sections you touched. A doc that describes a design the code no longer has is worse than no doc, because the next session trusts it.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1824:### [2026-08-17] #858 — `agent_runs.subject_id` is TEXT: a uuid compare against it is case-SENSITIVE while every `::uuid` cast normalises
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:1945:FAIL → the ticket promised refusals would be "durably queryable" via `completion_reason`. `ArchivalPoller.ts:60` deletes completed `job_outbox` rows 7 days after `completed_at`, and a policy refusal is *completed* (skip-as-success), never failed — so it takes the 7-day arm, not the 90-day dead-letter one. The adversarial review axis found it; three plan rounds and two implementation agents did not. WHY → the whole `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON` vocabulary inherits the archival life of the table it lives on. `owner_unreachable` (ADR 0010) has always had the same 7-day life, so nobody had ever had cause to state the limit. CORRECT → before promising durability from a `job_outbox` column, read the archival arm for that row's terminal status. If the question being answered outlives 7 days ("did we import without consent?"), the answer belongs on the domain row (`businesses.import_decision`), not the dispatch row. When the implementation is right and only the CLAIM is wrong, fix the claim — do not expand scope into a second sink; here that would have contradicted a locked non-goal and coupled the shared archival arm to one domain, the exact coupling the same ticket's ADR rejects. Confirmed live on staging: the 10 pre-existing `zernio_initial_backfill` completed rows all carry NULL, so NULL genuinely does not mean "genuine import".
