.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:3368:FAIL → the account-purge verifier's `businesses` branch is 20-odd `OR col IS NOT NULL` residue checks, so `tone` was about to be written the same way. `tone` is the ONE column the tombstone sets to a literal (`'professional and friendly'`) instead of nulling, which already makes it `<>` rather than `IS NOT NULL` — and `<>` is still wrong. WHY → `tone` is nullable, and `NULL <> 'professional and friendly'` evaluates to NULL, not TRUE. A row whose `tone` had been nulled — the exact residue state a detector exists to catch — passes a `<>` predicate silently. The idiom of the surrounding lines is what makes this invisible: every neighbour is a NULL-check, so the one line that must be NULL-*safe* reads like the odd one out rather than the careful one. CORRECT → `IS DISTINCT FROM` for any predicate whose expected value is a non-NULL literal on a nullable column. Mutation-proven both ways: drifting the literal fails the isolated case, and downgrading the operator to `<>` fails the deliberately-added `tone (nulled)` case. General rule: a residue predicate list has TWO shapes — "was cleared" and "was set to X" — and only the second can be defeated by NULL.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:3370:### [2026-08-18] The purge verifier is a GATE, not a report — it throws inside the finalize transaction
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:3371:FAIL → the #665 fix was reasoned about as "a detector was incomplete", which under-rates it in one direction and over-rates the risk of tightening it in the other. WHY → `verifyAccountOffboardingPurge` is called inside `finalizePurgeInTransaction` and a non-empty issue list `throw`s, rolling the whole finalize back. So a blind predicate is an OPEN GATE, not a missing metric — and, symmetrically, a newly-strict predicate that false-positives BLOCKS a legitimate purge rather than logging a warning. CORRECT → before widening this predicate list, confirm the write side already satisfies every new column across all reachable versions (here it had for many releases, so version skew cannot false-positive), and prefer a predicate that can only fire when the write side is genuinely broken. Corollary for the branch's one non-residue check: `archived_at IS NULL` is a violation because the tombstone SETS it (`COALESCE(archived_at, NOW())`) — that is the only predicate in the branch that can catch a row the tombstone never touched at all, because an untouched row whose optional columns happen to be empty looks purged to every residue check.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:3377:FAIL → the #665 test restores the whole five-column attestation tuple and asserts one violation. Plan review round 1 correctly rejected an earlier version: because migration 099's CHECK makes those five all-NULL or all-present, that test passes against an implementation probing only ONE of them. The accepted fix was a SQL-shape assertion pinning all five predicate names. That closes the naming gap and leaves a second one open. WHY → a shape assertion proves a predicate is PRESENT, never that its COMPARISON is right. `b.tone IS DISTINCT FROM 'wrong literal'` satisfies `toContain('b.tone IS DISTINCT FROM')` while detecting nothing, and a combined restore hides it because a sibling column's violation carries the count. CORRECT → where no constraint forces columns to move together, restore each column ALONE, assert exactly one violation, revert, and assert zero — the revert doubles as an assertion that the expected post-purge state is the one the verifier calls clean. Label each assertion with its column (`expect(actual, 'tone')`) so the failure names the culprit. Use the shape-assertion form only where a CHECK genuinely forbids isolation, and say so in the test.
.claude/reference/lab-notes.md:3458:FAIL → #900's intake declared `docs/directory.md` as the only registry the feature would touch. Three separate `allowed_edit_surface` amendments followed, each discovered as a red gate mid-task: `src/lib/agents/run-spine.ts` (`STEP_PERSONA` carries no `approval_downgraded` key, and `AgentStepKey` derives from it, so the call site does not typecheck), `scripts/ci/tenant-scope-allowlist.ts` (a platform-wide scan cannot bind a tenant id, and the audit proves scope only from a value-bound predicate), and `src/lib/approval/__tests__/outcome-anchor-pairing.test.ts` (the trap the #899 entry above records for ONE caller — #900 hit it as the fourth member of a set). The abort budget fired on the second amendment and would have torn down work that was on estimate and had passed three adversarial plan-review rounds. WHY → all four are *registries of permission*, not code: each answers "may this new module do the thing it is about to do", and none is discoverable by reading the module you are writing — only by running the audit that rejects you. They are also invisible to grep-by-feature, because the thing being added does not exist yet. CORRECT → **when a feature adds a background loop that both scans across accounts AND writes the run spine, declare all four in the intake up front**: `docs/directory.md`, `STEP_PERSONA`, `tenant-scope-allowlist.ts`'s `background-global-scan`, and `outcome-anchor-pairing.test.ts`'s `CALLER_ALLOWLIST`. Their siblings in the same family — `cost-guard-contract`, `admin-aal2`, `analytics-events`, `purge-verification` — govern other module classes; check which the feature actually triggers rather than assuming none.
