docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:9:I would request an approach change before merge, but not a rewrite. The branch has two strong architectural choices: a dedicated correction ledger is more proportionate than forcing accountless compliance events into `account_audit_log`, and the single data-modifying CTE is a sound way to make delete-plus-audit indivisible. The material weakness is the *contents* of the correction ledger. P-2 treats avoiding the offboarding inventory as evidence that tenant provenance should not exist, when that inventory is supposed to follow the data model rather than define it. The implementation then goes further and codifies a column spelling chosen to avoid detection (`src/lib/shared/__tests__/migration-098-suppression-corrections.integration.test.ts:185`).
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:11:The recommended shape is still a dedicated, immutable, FK-free table, but it should preserve the deleted row's relevant before-image: a nullable originating account identifier plus the original creator and note. It should then be deliberately classified in offboarding policy. Separately, owner marking should receive a durable account audit event; the analytics event is not an equivalent audit record.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:17:### Finding: the offboarding detector is driving the schema instead of checking it — HIGH
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:19:P-2 presents two supposedly independent reasons to omit `account_id`: indefinite retention, and the fact that the offboarding detector would then require a disposition (`docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-645-manual-suppression-paths.md:22`). Reason (b) is tail-wagging-dog. A detector is useful when it forces every account-linked record to have a deliberate lifecycle. It is not a reason to make an account-linked fact unrecognizable to the detector.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:21:The implementation makes that inversion unusually explicit. The migration says that adding the column would require inventory and purge verification (`supabase/migrations/098_solicitation_suppression_corrections.sql:21`), while the schema test asserts both the omission and that `removed_by_user_id` is deliberately named so it does not match the detector's `user_id` pattern (`src/lib/shared/__tests__/migration-098-suppression-corrections.integration.test.ts:185`). That is no longer merely avoiding a false positive. It is testing that a generic governance control does *not* see known identity provenance.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:25:An auditor investigating a removed global opt-out does not always need an originating account: `admin_manual` and some `provider_suppressed` rows legitimately have none. But when one existed, it is relevant evidence. It identifies the sending context that produced the revocation and distinguishes otherwise similar account-scoped rows. The design of record already takes that position for live global rows: it says their `account_id` is provenance, should survive offboarding intact, and can safely point to a tombstoned account (`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-suppression-unsubscribe-design.md:520`, `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-suppression-unsubscribe-design.md:528`). P-2's claim that the provenance ceases to matter merely because an administrator corrected the row conflicts with that established retention model.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:31:Keep the new table and keep it free of foreign keys, but add a nullable snapshot such as `originating_account_id UUID` (or simply `account_id`) copied from `DELETE ... RETURNING`. No FK and no cascade are appropriate because the audit record must outlive both the suppression and potentially the account. Add the table explicitly to the account-offboarding inventory with a scope-aware policy:
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:34:- for a corrected account-scoped owner preference, either scrub the account identifier during offboarding or retain only a tombstoned identifier, depending on the policy choice made explicitly for this audit class;
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:37:That is the third option the plan did not consider: **snapshot provenance without referential coupling, then classify the snapshot by policy**. It preserves the sound “no FK” decision without erasing useful context. Cost: one migration expansion, CTE/DTO/query/UI updates, and a new offboarding disposition plus purge verification. That is moderate work, but it is exactly the governance work the detector exists to demand.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:47:I do not recommend replacing `solicitation_suppression_corrections` with `account_audit_log`. The existing table structurally represents account-owned lifecycle events: `account_id` is non-null and cascades on account deletion (`supabase/migrations/034_multi_location_schema.sql:62`), and the helper requires an account on every write (`src/lib/audit/log.ts:24`). Making it accept accountless global compliance records would require changing its core ownership semantics, RLS assumptions, helper contract, offboarding behavior, and every consumer's interpretation. That is broader and riskier than adding a small domain audit table.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:104:3. Add an explicit offboarding classification and scope-aware retention/scrub verification for the correction table; strengthen the detector so identity-column naming cannot silently exempt a table.
docs/reviews/feat-645-manual-suppression-paths/codex-adversarial-approach-review.md:111:HIGH | `supabase/migrations/098_solicitation_suppression_corrections.sql:21` | P-2 lets offboarding-detector avoidance drive the schema and makes account-scoped corrections lose their originating account.
