docs/directory.md:812:| `vocabulary.ts` | The two-axis refusal vocabulary, pure — no DB, no clock. Axis A `REFUSAL_CATEGORIES`: the closed seven in precedence order, on the **Withhold reason** model (`WITHHOLD_REASONS` — `as const` array, union derived from it, `REFUSAL_CATEGORY_META` keyed `Record<ClosedEnum, Meta>` so a drifted enum is a compile error). Axis B `REFUSAL_SOURCES`: six lanes (`held_draft`, `auto_post_revoked`, `autonomy_suspended`, `ask_withheld`, `outreach_withheld`, `batch_halted`) — the map is PER-LANE because one code means different things in different lanes. 42 source codes: 25 to a category, 17 to explicit `null`. Traps: `null` is the recorded decision "not a refusal", NEVER an omission — the test uses `Object.hasOwn`, not truthiness; `refusalCategoryFor` THROWS `UnknownRefusalCodeError` rather than defaulting, because a default files an unmapped refusal under a plausible heading and the owner cannot tell the difference; `admin_halt`/`platform_halt` map to `autonomy_paused` on `batch_halted` but to `null` on `outreach_withheld` (`latchHalt` copies a batch halt through to every cancelled row, so counting both reports one halt again per recipient); `held_draft` has NO code axis, since `flagged_responses.reason` is moderation prose that may never leave the server. Four vocabularies are `satisfies Record<SourceEnum, RefusalCategory or null>`-guarded; `BATCH_HALT_REASONS` and `OUTREACH_WITHHELD_REASONS` have no TypeScript owner — their authority is the migration 111 CHECK, so a test parses `supabase/migrations/111_platform_halt_reason.sql` instead. Also exports `RENDER_WITHHOLD_REASONS` for the reason-dependent S6 anchor. **Keep this file `import type`-only.** It is imported by `'use client'` components for `REFUSAL_CATEGORY_META`, and a value import whose graph reaches `node:crypto` — as `@/lib/approval/audit` does — drags it into the browser bundle. No ESLint rule catches that pair; invariant #1's list does not cover it. This is why the S3 audit-action key space lives in `read.ts` and not here. |
docs/directory.md:813:| `read.ts` | The one owner-safe read: `getRefusalLog(sql, scope, window)` — same three-parameter shape as `getAgentTeamSummary`, same `DashboardReadScope` discriminant (renamed from `AgentTeamScope`, so both owner reads share one scope vocabulary), same `dashboardWindowParams` handling. ONE statement: `SELECT … FROM ( <arm> UNION ALL <arm> … ) AS refusal_entries ORDER BY occurred_at DESC, entry_id DESC` — one wrapper, one sort, one five-column set (`entry_id, source, code, occurred_at, review_id`), issued sequentially and never through a `Promise` combinator. Adding an arm: alias its root `r`, spell `r.account_id = ${scope.accountId}` in the arm, add `AND ${inLocation}` as its OWN conjunct (omit for account-wide S4), project the five columns. Traps: `ci:tenant-scope` substitutes one `$N` per `${}` in the raw template text, so a fragment placed straight after the account predicate reads as `r.account_id = $1 $2` and the arm is reported UNSCOPED — hence the standalone `AND ${inLocation}` / `TRUE` conjunct; a backtick inside a SQL comment TERMINATES the tagged template (cost a cross-task `TS1005`); because the arms share one statement, a structural assertion over the combined SQL cannot prove any single arm is scoped, so every arm carries its own behavioural foreign-account/foreign-location negative-row test; the window needs an UPPER bound the `getAgentTeamSummary` precedent lacks. Never selects `flagged_responses.reason` — not projected, not filtered on, not logged. Also the **server-only home of the S3 audit-action key space**: the five `AUDIT_ACTION_*` value imports, the reason→action table, `deriveActionCategories` and `ConflictingRefusalActionError`. That lives here rather than in `vocabulary.ts` because `@/lib/approval/audit` reaches `node:crypto` and the vocabulary is client-imported. The DB stores five audit ACTIONS where `AutoPolicyDowngradeReason` has six members (`DOWNGRADE_MARKERS` collapses `judge_disabled` and `judge_unhealthy`), so the action map is DERIVED from the reason map rather than written twice, and a collapse whose two reasons carry different categories throws. Consequently `refusalCategoryFor` alone cannot resolve an action without a cycle: `resolveRefusalCategory` (exported here) is base-lookup plus the one S3 step, delegating unchanged for every other lane. Injected `Sql` (invariant #15) — the invariant test is a whole-file substring scan, so even prose naming `@/lib/db/server` fails it. |
docs/directory.md:967:| `batch-view.ts` | **(#544)** Read model for the Requests screen: `readLatestBatch` (discriminated `no_batch` / `building` / `built` — funnel totals + a bounded per-reason evidence sample, all inside ONE `REPEATABLE READ, READ ONLY` transaction so an offboarding purge cannot yield a "built" batch with zero asks) + `markBatchReviewed` (idempotent first-write-wins `reviewed_at`, `GREATEST(NOW(), built_at)` against migration 088's CHECK). `parseEvidence` is the `solicitation_asks.evidence` PgBouncer JSONB parse site — CLAUDE.md allowlist. Timestamps read as `::text` (postgres.js truncates µs). Injected `Sql` (invariant #15). |
docs/directory.md:968:| `batch-builder.ts` | **(#593)** Batch build lane: `constructBatch` (per-business `pg_advisory_xact_lock` FIRST, then the `FOR UPDATE OF sb` row lock — one global order, advisory-before-row, so purge and construction cannot deadlock ABBA) + `prepareBatch`. Candidate selection, restraint evaluation and the ask fan-out land in one tx with the `built_at` CAS. Injected `Sql` (invariant #15). |
docs/directory.md:969:| `outreach.ts` | **(#624)** Send-lane lifecycle kernel: `deriveBatchState` (the read-time closed union over `solicitation_batches`' monotone timestamps — there is deliberately no `status` column), `closeBatchIfSettled` (the single owner of batch closure: CASes `send_closed_at` on NULL, records one close step, terminalizes the **Send batch parent** through an ordered disposition precedence — every write gated on the winning CAS because `appendRunStep` always appends), and the approval/send window boundaries. Also the single owner of `canonicalBatchId` **(X18, the 2026-08-22 architecture pass)** — the lower-case form `agent_runs.subject_id`'s TEXT compare needs, since every `::uuid` cast normalises and that column (migration 071) does not. It lives here because `closeBatchIfSettled` already lowercased the same key and both other callers (`approval.ts`, `ask-generation.ts`) already import this file. Two standing rules: canonicalise ONCE at a public entry point and NEVER at a SQL site (per-site lowercasing is how this recurred), and never unify the send-parent QUERY — `findActiveSolicitationSendBatchRun` asks *is there an ACTIVE parent*, the rest ask *the LATEST parent*, and they are different questions. Disposition arrives as an explicit `failure` parameter, never read back off `agent_runs.error`, so batch→run is the sole lock order. Injected `Sql`/`TransactionSql` (invariant #15). |
docs/directory.md:970:| `approval.ts` | **(#629; #860, #865 and #872 each add a verb)** The five verbs of the send lane a human triggers, owner and RepWorth operator alike — deliberately not in `outreach.ts`, whose interface is the machine lifecycle and has no actor in any signature. Since **(#900)** the file also carries exactly ONE actor-less verb, `latchSystemHalt`, and it lives here rather than in `outreach.ts` because it shares the private `latchHalt` body with the two human halt arms — the shared body is what keeps all three off `sending` rows. `approveBatch` (readiness gate → exclusions **validated before any write**, because postgres.js commits on a normal return and a late refusal would otherwise commit a partial cancellation → CAS on `approved_at IS NULL` → `veto_deadline_at = +15 min` → run step → `closeBatchIfSettled`) · `haltBatch` and `adminHaltBatch`, the owner and admin arms of the **Halt latch**, both thin over the private `latchHalt` — CAS on `halted_at IS NULL` → cancel `rendered`/`queued` and **never `sending`** → the audit row → the spine step → `closeBatchIfSettled`, in that order, so a second halt adds neither receipt nor step and a throwing audit write rolls the latch back with it; the admin arm fixes its reason at `admin_halt` (no request field can name it), requires a note, writes `solicitation_batch_admin_halted` to `account_audit_log` in the same tx, and refuses a `pending` batch, whose latch passes 091:176 and then breaks the builder's later `built_at` stamp (both verbs refuse it since #865 step-7 review — the path was API-reachable with an id the backend itself returns, so the guard is a lifecycle invariant rather than a UI affordance) · `latchSystemHalt` (**(#900)** the **Halt latch**'s THIRD arm, and the only writer here with no actor at all — `actorUserId` is `null` because migration 091's halt-consistency CHECK REQUIRES `halted_by IS NULL` for exactly the two system reasons `send_window_elapsed` and `batch_expired`, so the type says what the database enforces. Its callers are the release cycle's two batch sweeps (`src/lib/solicitation/release.ts` phases 2 and 3), never a route. It takes the batch ROW lock itself via the shared `readBatchForAction` (`FOR UPDATE OF b`) and deliberately skips only the ADVISORY lock, which the sweeps never take — so no cycle exists against the advisory-then-row order. It applies NO guard list: the `pending` refusal the two human arms carry is a UI-reachability invariant, and a sweep selecting on timestamps cannot reach a `pending` batch. Writes no `account_audit_log` row, because that log records a human acting on an account and filing a clock there would make it lie about who acted) · `requestAskRegeneration` (**(#860)** **Ask regeneration**'s request half: writes no domain state, only the `job_outbox` row and the step recording who asked; lives here rather than beside `regenerateAsk` because since ADR 0020 its ENTIRE refusal vocabulary is approval-lane lifecycle) · `withdrawAsk` (**(#872)** **Ask withdrawal**: the **Halt latch** at ask granularity — same lock order (advisory batch → batch row → outreach row → spine), same row predicate `rendered`/`queued` and **never `sending`**, ONE row → `cancelled`/`owner_withdrawn` (migration 109); the decision is the pure `classifyWithdrawal(state, currentAttempt)`, idempotence checked BEFORE the state gate so a retry after a last-row close answers `already_withdrawn`; validate-before-write; `recordStep('ask_withdrawn')` then `closeBatchIfSettled`, so the last live row settles the batch `exhausted`) · `ownerHaltReason` (derived server-side from **Batch state**, never read from the request — a client that could name its own reason could file a mid-drip halt as a veto). Lock order for the four approval/latch verbs is batch → run (amendment B11), so every run-spine call happens last. **B11 is INCOMPLETE for a writer that owns an outbox attempt**, and `requestAskRegeneration` is one: it prefixes the `job_outbox` row AHEAD of that chain (`job_outbox` → advisory batch → `solicitation_batches` → `solicitation_outreach` → `agent_runs`), because `regenerateAsk`'s swap opens with `markOutboxCompletedIfOwned` and taking the advisory lock first would deadlock against the worker it is enqueueing for. Enqueue-first forces validate-after-mutate, so its refusals **throw** `RegenRequestAbort` rather than returning — a normal return would commit the outbox row; do not "tidy" it back to validate-first. Its send-parent lookup is a correlated scalar subquery and its readiness gate a `NOT EXISTS`, both carrying their own tenant keys — the natural shapes #629 D7 had to avoid until `ci:tenant-scope` learned to read query blocks (#859); do not split them back out for the audit's sake. Two traps that used to live in CLAUDE.md's routing row and belong here instead: **approval readiness is `non-cancelled`, NOT `rendered`** — a `withheld` row is a FINISHED attempt with nothing left to render, so requiring `rendered` would wedge every batch that had one lint failure; and `requestAskRegeneration` **enqueues BEFORE it validates and THROWS its refusals** rather than returning them, so a caller that only reads the return value sees nothing. Injected `Sql`/`TransactionSql` (invariant #15). |
docs/directory.md:1045:| `held-draft.ts` | The held-draft gate: `openFlagExistsSql(db, ref)` — the one composable spelling of "response has an OPEN `flagged_responses` row" (`'open'` is a literal, matching migration 051's partial index byte-for-byte) — `hasOpenFlag(tx, responseId)` (in-tx, post-lock), and `HeldForReviewError`. Every owner-facing release path must call it; admin, health-alert, purge and producer consumers stay deliberately separate (#725). |
docs/directory.md:1103:| `email/templates/account-offboarding.ts` | `buildAccountOffboardingEmailHtml(params)` — one builder for the seven offboarding owner emails, switched on `emailType`: cancel_only confirmation (default) / access-end / reactivation confirmation, delete_data confirmation (requires `keepMyDataUrl`, shows `purgeAfter`), purge reminder, keep-my-data confirmation, and the purge receipt, whose retention sentence renders only when `retainedSuppressionCount > 0` **(#643)**. |
docs/directory.md:1148:| `stripe-error-inspect.ts` | Single owner of the STRIPE error-inspection vocabulary: `RETRYABLE_STRIPE_ERROR_TYPES`, `PERMANENT_STRIPE_ERROR_TYPES`, and `isStripeCouponError` (classifier only — `param` → `code` → `message`). The provider-agnostic half (`RETRYABLE_NETWORK_ERROR_CODES`, `errorStringProperty` / `errorNumberProperty`) moved to `shared/error-inspect.ts` (#913). Retry POLICY stays with the two consumers (`account-offboarding/stripe-invoices.ts`, `worker/jobs/billing/stripe-overage-error.ts`). |
docs/directory.md:1197:| `offboardings-service.ts` | **(Phase 3)** `getAdminOffboardings(sql, {limit, offset})` — the lifecycle list + count, the row mapper, and `scrubAdminLastError` (the security-relevant redactor). Exports the `AdminOffboarding`/`AdminProviderDisposition` DTOs the client imports. Single owner shared by `GET /api/admin/offboardings` and `offboardings/page.tsx`. |
docs/directory.md:1238:### `account-offboarding/`
docs/directory.md:1240:Owner offboarding domain (`cancel_only` / `delete_data` / purge) — the confirm, gate, keep-my-data, reactivation, disposition and purge-verification modules behind `/api/account/offboarding/*`, the admin disposition route, and the `worker/jobs/account-offboarding/` workers; design of record per CLAUDE.md is `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-06-05-owner-offboarding-design.md`. All modules take an injected `sql`/`tx` (invariant #15) and enqueue only via `outbox.ts`.
docs/directory.md:1244:| `analytics.ts` | Four typed wrappers over `enqueueAnalyticsEvent` for the offboarding lifecycle: `enqueueOffboardingStarted`, `enqueueOffboardingReactivated`, `enqueueOffboardingTeardownCompleted`, `enqueueOffboardingPurged`. Event keys derive from the offboarding id; the purged event keys and identifies by a SHA-256 hash of that id and carries no account/user id. Take the caller's `sql`/`tx`. |
docs/directory.md:1245:| `confirm.ts` | `confirmCancelOnlyOffboarding` / `confirmDeleteDataOffboarding` — the owner-confirm transactions. Each hashes the idempotency key, resolves an existing active row (idempotent replay or `duplicate_active`), captures the snapshot, inserts `account_offboardings` as `pending` (delete_data adds `purge_after = NOW() + 30 days` and disconnects every live Zernio connection on the account), writes audit + analytics, and enqueues billing-schedule (cancel_only) or teardown + confirmation email (delete_data) — one tx. |
docs/directory.md:1247:| `email-dedup.ts` | `accountOffboardingDeliveryId(offboardingId, emailType)` — deterministic UUIDv5-shaped `notification_deliveries` id (SHA-256 of namespace + offboarding id + email type) — and `accountOffboardingEmailSendIdempotencyKey(deliveryId)` for the Resend send. `AccountOffboardingEmailDeliveryType` adds `purge_receipt` to the payload's email types. |
docs/directory.md:1249:| `gate.ts` | Offboarding-aware access gate. `getOffboardingState(accountId, db)` loads the latest non-terminal `account_offboardings` row; `activeOffboardingStateFromProjection` maps a joined `offboarding_*` projection; `createOffboardingGate(db)` memoizes per request. `isOffboardingIntentBlocked(state, intent)` over `OFFBOARDING_INTENTS` plus per-intent `is*Blocked` helpers: delete_data / tearing_down / purge_scheduled / most `failed` rows block everything; a cancel_only pre-teardown `scheduled` or billing-failure row freezes only checkout + billing-expanding mutations. `lockAccountBeforeOffboardingProjection` pins the account row first. |
docs/directory.md:1250:| `keep-my-data.ts` | Cancels a scheduled delete_data purge. `keepMyDataForAccount` (owner session + idempotency key) and `keepMyDataWithToken` (raw emailed token, hashed and consumed under `FOR UPDATE`; rejects invalid / expired / consumed / revoked). Both run `applyKeepMyData`: stamps `purge_cancelled_at`, flips a teardown-completed row to `churned` + terminal, revokes remaining keep-my-data tokens, writes `account_offboarding_keep_my_data` audit and enqueues `keep_my_data_confirmation` — one tx. |
docs/directory.md:1251:| `outbox.ts` | Transactional-outbox enqueue cluster for the five `account_offboarding_*` topics: `enqueueAccountOffboardingBillingSchedule`, `…Email`, `…Reactivation`, `…Teardown`, `…Purge`. Every function takes the caller's `tx` and binds JSONB via `tx.json` (no `@/lib/db/server` import). Email and reactivation ids are deterministic (SHA-256 namespace + parts) with `ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING`; the purge-receipt email carries recipient / `purged_at` / `retained_suppression_count` in the payload because the row is scrubbed by then **(#643)**. |
docs/directory.md:1252:| `provider-disposition.ts` | Admin manual resolution of a provider resource teardown could not reach. `ProviderDispositionInputSchema` (zod); `buildAdminProviderDispositionRecord` scrubs text and hashes the resource reference; `recordProviderDisposition(input, db)` appends to `provider_dispositions` under `FOR UPDATE`, writes the audit row, and — only when a `failed`/`teardown` row's every applicable provider step is now covered — transitions it to `purge_scheduled`. `unresolvedApplicableTeardownProviders` derives applicability from the snapshot, never the step column. |
docs/directory.md:1253:| `purge-sentinels.ts` | `PURGE_TOMBSTONE_SENTINELS` (#892) — the nine values the purge tombstone WRITES and the purge verifier CHECKS, so the two agree by construction rather than because the same strings were typed twice each. Values only, never SQL: each side composes its own predicate and keeps its own operator (writer `=`, verifier `IS DISTINCT FROM`), which is where a reader needs to see the asymmetry. The two jsonb sentinels are semantic `[]` / `{}` bound through `tx.json` / `exec.json`, not JSON text plus a cast. Provenance is per value and always a SCHEMA fact: `tone` is `001:71`'s column default and explicitly NOT `prompt-builder.ts`'s `DEFAULT_TONE`. The email is two fragments because it wraps a per-row id; neither contains `%`, `_` or a backslash, which is what lets the verifier compare it with `IS DISTINCT FROM` instead of `LIKE`. |
docs/directory.md:1254:| `purge-verification.ts` | `verifyAccountOffboardingPurge(input, exec)` — post-purge read-side check run inside the finalize tx: one `verificationQuery` per `PURGE_VERIFICATION_TABLES` entry counting surviving or unscrubbed rows for the account (tombstone shape, absent rows, scrubbed payloads, `distinct_id` residue). Fail-closed twice over: an inventoried table without a branch throws `missing_purge_verification_sql`, and the whole check runs INSIDE `finalizePurgeInTransaction` and throws on any violation — it is a gate, not a report, so a blind predicate is an open gate and a false-positive one blocks a legitimate purge. The `businesses` branch verifies all **28** tombstone-state columns (28 since #665) and deliberately excludes the bookkeeping `updated_at` assignment, so the writer makes 29 assignments against the branch's 28 predicates. **Three** of the file's predicates are not residue checks: `tone` and `tracked_keywords` compare against a RESTORED schema default, and `accounts.flags` does the same in the `accounts` branch; separately `archived_at IS NULL` is the branch's only PRESENCE check, a violation because the tombstone SETS it. Every tombstone-sentinel comparison uses `IS DISTINCT FROM` and every sentinel VALUE comes from `purge-sentinels.ts` (#892) — that file's header owns the reasoning for both. `absentByBusiness` is the allowlisted by-business COUNT twin of the purge's `deleteByBusiness`. |
docs/directory.md:1256:| `reactivation-request.ts` | `requestCancelOnlyReactivation(sql, input)` — the route-side half: locks the latest reactivatable `scheduled` cancel_only row (`FOR UPDATE`), requires a `stripe_subscription_id`, and enqueues `account_offboarding_reactivation` with the hashed idempotency key. Returns `queued` or `not_reactivatable`; the Stripe call happens in `ReactivationWorker`. |
docs/directory.md:1257:| `retry-billing.ts` | `retryCancelOnlyBillingSchedule(sql, input)` — self-service recovery for a cancel_only row stuck at `failed` / `failed_step='billing'` (BullMQ retries exhausted, subscription never cancelled — a non-terminal row that blocks a fresh offboarding). Locks account + row, resets to `pending` (`stripe_step='not_applicable'`, `retry_count+1`), writes `account_offboarding_billing_retry` audit and re-enqueues the billing-schedule job in one tx. |
docs/directory.md:1258:| `scrub-error.ts` | `scrubOffboardingErrorForStorage(message)` — single owner of the string-side redaction applied before an offboarding error is persisted (`last_error`, Sentry `extra`, rethrown messages): emails, phone numbers, Bearer/Basic credentials, `token=` / `"secret":`-style pairs, truncated to 500 chars. Object payloads use `shared/sentry-scrub.ts` instead. |
docs/directory.md:1260:| `status.ts` | `getAccountOffboardingStatus(accountId, db)` — owner-facing read model: the active offboarding (id, mode, status, failed step, access-end / teardown / purge timestamps) plus `operationalUIBlocked` from `gate.ts`, or `activeOffboarding: null`. |
docs/directory.md:1262:| `table-disposition.ts` | The per-table purge inventory: `TABLE_DISPOSITIONS` (48 tables), each with a disposition (`hard_delete`, `scrub_in_place`, `retain_deidentified`, `retain_compliance_limited_pii`, `not_applicable`), scope evidence, purge-verification mode and reason; `ALLOWED_PURGE_VERIFICATION_BY_DISPOSITION` pins which modes a disposition may use. Evidence + verification input only — the executable purge SQL lives in `worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts`. Derives `ACCOUNT_OFFBOARDING_TABLES` and `PURGE_VERIFICATION_TABLES`. |
docs/directory.md:1264:| `types.ts` | Type-only vocabulary: `AccountOffboardingMode` (`cancel_only` / `delete_data`), `AccountOffboardingStatus` (8 values) split into terminal (`purged` / `churned` / `reactivated`) and active subsets, `AccountOffboardingFailedStep` (`billing` / `teardown` / `purge`), `AccountOffboardingStepOutcome` (`success` / `failed` / `not_applicable`), and the `ActiveOffboardingState` shape `gate.ts` returns. |
docs/directory.md:1490:| `AutonomySuspendedNoticeWorker.ts` | Account-level owner-notice orchestrator. Re-checks the current suspension event, subscription/offboarding posture, and owner channels, dispatches without review/reply content, and keeps a live delivery claim retryable. |
docs/directory.md:1491:| `context.ts` | Loads the suspension audit event, account, delivery-anchor business, and verified enrollments; guard-and-return branches make stale/mismatched/offboarded notices harmless. |
docs/directory.md:1514:| `context.ts` | Loads escalation + review + business rows with escalation-specific guards: not-found/id-mismatch → fail; `resolved` → skip (moot); archived/offboarding → skip. Triage snapshot comes from the `review_escalations` row — no LLM call. |
docs/directory.md:1523:| `context.ts` | Loads the posted `auto_policy` response + business rows; skip guards (not found/mismatch → fail; not posted / not auto-policy / archived / offboarding → skip). |
docs/directory.md:1534:| `context.ts` | Loads response + review + business rows with veto-window freshness guards: not found/mismatch → fail; no longer `approved`/`auto_policy` (vetoed, kill-switch-downgraded, posted, superseded) → skip; `auto_post_scheduled_at` NULL or already passed → skip (stale backlog — an unactionable notice is noise); archived/offboarding → skip. Deadline staleness is compared IN SQL against the same DB clock that stamped it. |
docs/directory.md:1545:| `context.ts` | Loads business+account rows FIRST (unlike its siblings, which load their subject first) — `account_id` isn't on the payload, so the batch lookup below needs it to be tenant-scoped by BOTH `account_id` AND `business_id`. Skip guards: archived / offboarding → skip; batch not found (incl. cross-tenant, indistinguishable by the scoped WHERE) → fail; `approval_method != 'auto_policy'` / halted / `send_closed_at` → skip. Anchored is deliberately NOT a skip — a retry must still claim any channel that hasn't delivered, and the **Veto anchor** is computed FROM this notice's own `sent_at`. Reads N (live rendered Outreach count) fresh at send time, not off the payload. |
docs/directory.md:1563:| `load-context.ts` | `loadPublishResponseContext` — parallel pool reads of the response row and the business⋈account⋈active-offboarding projection; response not found / `business_id` mismatch / business not found → `markOutboxFailed` and `'terminal'`. Returns `PublishResponseContext`. |
docs/directory.md:1580:| `load-context.ts` | `loadGenerationContext` — pre-generation gate: reads review + business rows, builds `NormalizedReview` / `BusinessContext`, runs the subscription and offboarding guards, and performs the idempotency check. Returns `LoadOutcome` (`ready` / `review_not_found` / `business_not_found` / `skipped` / `already_exists`); merges `account_id` into the log context. No outbox writes. |
docs/directory.md:1589:| `job-eligibility.ts` | Shared pre-flight gate for both Zernio job workers. `guardZernioJobEligibility()` — one `businesses ⋈ accounts` row, then the project's archived-before-subscription guard order; every skip is skip-as-success (outbox row COMPLETED, never failed or retried). **(#679)** `guardImportConsent()` — the fail-closed **Import consent** gate the BACKFILL worker runs first. Proceeds only on `accepted`; `pending`/`declined`/derived `expired`/missing row all log a structured `reason` and complete. Deliberately a SEPARATE export, not a `jobKind` branch, so the ingest worker never learns the interface. Its whole body runs in `sql.begin` with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` + `markOutboxCompletedWithReason(tx, …)` — both load-bearing against the accept-races-guard data-loss window — and derives expiry through the imported `importConsentStateSql` fragment, which is why its query MUST alias `businesses` as `b`. **(#695)** That completion stamps `job_outbox.completion_reason = 'import_consent_blocked'` (`OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON`; column from migration 083), so a policy refusal is separable by SQL from a genuine completed import for the 7-day archival window — an operational signal, not an audit record, and nothing reads it in code (ADR 0016). `guardZernioJobEligibility`'s three skips deliberately keep using plain `markOutboxCompleted`, leaving `completion_reason` NULL. |
docs/directory.md:1622:| `failed-hook.ts` | **(#899)** `handleSolicitationSendFailed` — exhaustion terminalizer for `solicitation_send`, and the ONLY writer of `send_exhausted` (`dispatch.ts`'s tx2 owns `send_failed`). Fires on the terminal attempt only, under the `'failed'`-EVENT predicate `attemptsMade >= max` — deliberately NOT `isTerminalAttempt`, the catch-block form, which fires one attempt early here. One transaction: `acquireSolicitationBatchLock` FIRST (advisory-before-row-locks, or ABBA against the offboarding purge), then the `markOutboxFailed` ownership CAS, the row read `FOR UPDATE`, the terminal CAS, the **Lazy child**'s `failed` terminal and `closeBatchIfSettled`. **THE ARRIVAL STATUS PICKS THE TERMINAL, and conflating the two is the trap:** `queued` (the attempt died above tx1's CAS, nothing was ever on the wire) ⇒ `failed`/`send_exhausted`; `sending` (the attempt died after the CAS, the message may already have reached the recipient) ⇒ `unknown`/`send_indeterminate`, shared with #900's D12d sweep. Writing `failed` for a `sending` row is the named lie-with-teeth — the restraint policy's `over_contacted` check counts prior SENDS, so a possible send recorded as "not sent" re-enrols that customer in a future batch and mails them twice. Both pairs are already legal under 091's status/reason CHECK (re-stated by 110), so the split needs no migration. The child terminalizes `failed` on BOTH arms — `agent_runs` has no `unknown`, and the indeterminacy is carried in `failure_class`. `boundedErrorClass` is imported from `solicitation-batch/failed-hook-shared.ts` (log-field hygiene, no lane semantics); `isSendAttemptExhausted` stays local because exhaustion semantics are the lane's own. A stale attempt, a vanished row or a row another writer already terminalized writes nothing further; a failed transaction leaves the outbox row `dispatched` for the watchdog. |
docs/directory.md:1631:| `UpdateStripeOverageWorker.ts` | Queue `update_stripe_overage_quantity` (`handleUpdateStripeOverage`) — under `acquireBillingLock` + `accounts FOR UPDATE` re-checks offboarding state, Stripe subscription, `subscriptionGrantsFullAccess`, tier/variant, then counts unarchived locations → `computeOverageQuantity` → `syncStripeOverageItem`. Maps the outcome onto ONE outbox effect: transient Postgres/Stripe errors leave the row `dispatched` (`recordOutboxTransientError`); terminal → `markOutboxFailed` + `UnrecoverableError`; `ambiguous` also pages Sentry. Concurrency 1. |
docs/directory.md:1632:| `overage-sync.ts` | `syncStripeOverageItem(stripe, input, logger)` — the Stripe interaction for one overage recompute: retrieve subscription → `resolveOverageItem` (confidence-graded) → exactly one mutation (delete/update/create). Returns `OverageSyncOutcome` (`applied | noop | policy_blocked | ambiguous`), honours `mutationPolicy: 'contractions_only'` during offboarding, throws raw Stripe errors for the caller to classify. Idempotency keys are per-outbox (`operationScope`) on purpose — a stable per-subscription key would 400 on delete→re-add. |
docs/directory.md:1633:| `stripe-overage-error.ts` | `classifyStripeOverageError(err)` → `transient | terminal`, the retry policy for `UpdateStripeOverageWorker`. Vocabulary comes from `src/lib/payments/stripe-error-inspect.ts`; the policy delta here: a 409 in-flight idempotency conflict is TRANSIENT (offboarding treats all idempotency errors as permanent), 400 `idempotency_error` terminal, 429/5xx/network codes transient, other 4xx and unknown terminal. |
docs/directory.md:1635:#### `worker/jobs/account-offboarding/` — offboarding workers
docs/directory.md:1637:Five BullMQ handlers for the `account_offboarding_*` topics enqueued by `src/lib/account-offboarding/outbox.ts`, plus the teardown step modules and purge stages they orchestrate. Every handler loads its `account_offboardings` row, skips already-terminal rows, rethrows on non-terminal attempts and marks `failed` + `failed_step` only on the terminal attempt; error text is redacted via `scrubOffboardingErrorForStorage`.
docs/directory.md:1641:| `AccountOffboardingEmailWorker.ts` | Queue `account_offboarding_email`. `handleAccountOffboardingEmail`: `EMAIL_DESCRIPTORS` (one entry per row-backed email type — template, subject, validation, body) drives load → validate → claim a deterministic `notification_deliveries` row → `sendInternalEmail` under a deterministic Resend idempotency key; `delete_data_confirmation` mints/reuses the keep-my-data token. Terminal-row emails null `recipient_email` after send (best-effort). `purge_receipt` is row-less: recipient comes from the payload, and outbox completion + payload scrub are one UPDATE. |
docs/directory.md:1642:| `BillingScheduleWorker.ts` | Queue `account_offboarding_billing_schedule`. `handleAccountOffboardingBillingSchedule`: for a `pending` cancel_only row, Stripe `subscriptions.update({cancel_at_period_end:true})` under a deterministic idempotency key, reads `current_period_end` (subscription item first, then the legacy top-level field), then in one tx sets `scheduled` + `access_ends_at` + `stripe_step='success'`, enqueues the confirmation email and completes the outbox. Terminal attempt marks `failed` / `failed_step='billing'` (recoverable via `retry-billing.ts`); Sentry `account_offboarding_billing_schedule_failed`. |
docs/directory.md:1643:| `PurgeWorker.ts` | Queue `account_offboarding_purge`. `handleAccountOffboardingPurge(sql, supabaseServer, job)`: `validatePurgeableOffboarding`, then the strictly ordered `purge-stages.ts` stages (auth binding → dependent refs → raw payloads → content → analytics → provider identifiers → audit/cost payloads → parent tombstone) and `finalizePurgeInTransaction`. Stale outbox attempts exit silently; retryable failures stamp `last_error` and rethrow; the terminal attempt marks `failed` / `failed_step='purge'`. Sentry `account_offboarding_purge_failed`. |
docs/directory.md:1644:| `ReactivationWorker.ts` | Queue `account_offboarding_reactivation`. Thin: calls `reactivateCancelOnlyOffboarding` with the payload's actor + idempotency-key hash, completes the outbox on success, otherwise fails it with `account_offboarding_stripe_reversal_unavailable` or `account_offboarding_reactivation_<reason>`. |
docs/directory.md:1645:| `TeardownWorker.ts` | Queue `account_offboarding_teardown`. `handleAccountOffboardingTeardown` for both modes: cancel_only first requires `access_ends_at` passed and Stripe reporting `canceled`; marks `tearing_down`, then runs `teardown-stripe` → `teardown-zernio` → `teardown-locations` → `teardown-outbox`, each idempotent on its `*_step` column. `finalizeTeardown` derives the terminal status from the LIVE `purge_cancelled_at` (delete_data → `purge_scheduled`, or `churned` if keep-my-data landed mid-flight; cancel_only → `churned` + `cancel_only_access_end` email). Terminal failures set `failed` / `failed_step='teardown'` plus the failing step column. |
docs/directory.md:1646:| `purge-helpers.ts` | Pure leaf helpers for the purge: `jobOutboxScrubScopeRecord` / `impersonationSessionScrubScopeRecord` (the scope records appended to `provider_dispositions`: id + SHA-256), `safeSupabaseError` / `isSupabaseAuthNotFound`, and `deleteByBusiness(tx, table, accountId)` — the allowlisted (`DELETABLE_BY_BUSINESS`) by-business DELETE so the dynamic table identifier is never untrusted. |
docs/directory.md:1647:| `purge-stages.ts` | The eight `run*PurgeStage` bodies, each its own `sql.begin` (auth binding = Supabase `deleteUser` then detach `users.auth_id`; content stage takes the csv-ingest + solicitation-batch advisory locks first **(#582, #624)**; raw-payload stage scrubs `inbound_events` / `job_outbox` by identifier scope), plus `finalizePurgeInTransaction` — locks the current outbox attempt, scrubs the row, runs `verifyAccountOffboardingPurge`, sets `purged` + terminal, enqueues the durable `purge_receipt` email **(#643)**. `validatePurgeableOffboarding` guards force-transitioned rows. |
docs/directory.md:1649:| `teardown-outbox.ts` | `runOutboxTerminalization(sql, row, currentOutboxId)` — completes still-`pending` `job_outbox` rows the account no longer needs: `generate-response` / `send-notification` / `publish-response` / `auto_post_notice` rows for the snapshotted locations, and sibling `account_offboarding_{billing_schedule,reactivation,teardown}` rows for this offboarding (never the running job); then stamps `job_terminalization_step='success'`. |
docs/directory.md:1669:| `poller-heartbeat.ts` | Idle-cycle log suppression with a liveness heartbeat, shared by the account-offboarding pollers (and `HealthAlertPoller`): `notePollerActivity(key)` records a real work line; `maybeLogPollerHeartbeat(logger, key)` emits one `Idle heartbeat` at most every 30 min of continuous idle, so an alive-but-idle loop stays observable and a dead one shows as a missing heartbeat. Per-process state; `__resetPollerHeartbeatState` is test-only. |
docs/directory.md:1677:| `ArchivalPoller.ts` | Archives old reviews, responses, and cost rows to keep the main tables fast. |
docs/directory.md:1687:| `AccountOffboardingAccessEndPoller.ts` | Every 60s (`ACCOUNT_OFFBOARDING_ACCESS_END_INTERVAL_MS`; batch ≤100): claims `scheduled` cancel_only rows whose `access_ends_at` has passed and that hold no reactivation claim (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`, one tx per row), sets `tearing_down` + `teardown_started_at`, writes `account_offboarding_teardown_started` audit and enqueues `account_offboarding_teardown`. Gated by `ACCOUNT_OFFBOARDING_ENABLED`; 45s cycle deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1688:| `AccountOffboardingPurgePoller.ts` | Every 60s: claims delete_data `purge_scheduled` rows past `purge_after` (not cancelled / not started) that either completed teardown or carry a concrete `admin_provider_disposition`; stamps `purge_started_at`, revokes open keep-my-data tokens, writes `account_offboarding_purge_started` audit and enqueues `account_offboarding_purge` — one tx per row. `getAccountOffboardingPurgeWindowOverrideMs` shortens the window for non-production dry-runs (capped at 3 days). Flag-gated; 45s deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1689:| `AccountOffboardingPurgeReminderPoller.ts` | Every 60s: for delete_data `purge_scheduled` rows within `REMINDER_LEAD_DAYS` (3) of `purge_after` with `purge_reminder_sent_at` unset, stamps it, writes `account_offboarding_purge_reminder_sent` audit and enqueues the `delete_data_purge_reminder` email in one tx. Flag-gated; 45s deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1690:| `AccountOffboardingReactivationResetPoller.ts` | Every 60s: finds cancel_only `scheduled` rows whose `reactivation_claimed_at` is >5 min old (the originating reactivation died mid-reversal), idempotently re-applies Stripe `cancel_at_period_end:true` under the same compensation key `reactivate.ts` uses, then releases the claim guarded by the observed token and writes `account_offboarding_reactivation_claim_reset` audit. Retryable Stripe errors defer; permanent ones release anyway. Flag-gated; 45s deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1691:| `AccountOffboardingTeardownRetryPoller.ts` | Every 60s: re-drives rows at `failed` / `failed_step='teardown'` (both modes) after a per-row cooldown (`ACCOUNT_OFFBOARDING_TEARDOWN_RETRY_COOLDOWN_MS`, default 5 min) and under `ACCOUNT_OFFBOARDING_TEARDOWN_MAX_RETRIES` (default 12, counted by `retry_count`): sets `tearing_down`, writes `account_offboarding_teardown_retry_scheduled` audit, enqueues `account_offboarding_teardown`. Teardown steps early-return on `success`, so only the failed step reruns. Flag-gated; 45s deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1692:| `CancellationGracePoller.ts` | Every 24h (`CANCELLATION_GRACE_INTERVAL_MS`): for `cancelled` accounts with `cancelled_at` older than 30 days and no `account_offboardings` row, soft-archives every remaining active location (`archived_at = NOW()`) under `acquireLocationLifecycleLock` + row locks and inserts a `location_soft_archived_grace_expired` row per location directly into `account_audit_log` (VAL-BILLING-048/049). Not flag-gated; 30-min cycle deadline **(#414)**. |
docs/directory.md:1789:| `subscription-deleted.ts` | `processSubscriptionDeleted(sub)` — loads every account under the customer; none → throws `AccountNotProvisionedYetError` (retry); acts on the one whose CURRENT `stripe_subscription_id` matches, skipping a subscription that only matches an active offboarding snapshot; then under the entitlement lock runs the applier (→ `cancelled`). |
docs/directory.md:1790:| `subscription-updated.ts` | `processSubscriptionUpdated(eventSnapshot)` → `SubscriptionUpdatedOutcome` (`updated | variant_mismatch | status_only | ignored`). Resolves the account by customer + subscription (stale/offboarding-snapshot subscriptions ignored; none → `AccountNotProvisionedYetError`), then in one tx under `acquireTierAndBillingLocks` retrieves the provider-current subscription, runs the status applier (a resolved no-op still enqueues mirror sync), resolves base/overage items via `resolveSubscriptionBilling` and writes the tier snapshot + `locations_paid`. Post-commit `synchronizeBusinessTierMirror`; alerts (unmapped price, variant mismatch, untrusted overage, Autopilot-while-unavailable) → Sentry. |
docs/directory.md:1804:| `solicitation-lane.ts` | **(#657, spec §5)** `isSolicitationLane(data)` (the tag discriminant, tolerant of Resend's object-map *and* array tag shapes) + `handleSolicitationResendEvent(sql, eventType, emailId, data)` over the seven subscribed events. **Write surface is deliberately narrow:** only `bounced_at`, `complained_at`, `provider_error` and the **Suppression ledger** — never `delivery_status` or `sent_at` (#630 owns the send lifecycle, and a webhook write would race migration 091's ladder CHECKs) and never `unsubscribed_at` (that is `worker/ingress/unsubscribe.ts`'s RFC 8058 POST). §5.3's `delivered_at` effect is dropped — no such column, no migration. `withAttribution` is the ONE transaction owner: the `FOR UPDATE OF o` attribution read, the outreach UPDATE and the optional ledger write are one commit or none, and it returns a value **only on a committed write** so nothing logs a fact the database does not hold. Unmatched ids split by event (**#967**): suppression-bearing ones (hard bounce, complaint, `email.suppressed`) write the recipient's **global** opt-out from the SIGNED payload with `accountId: null` and **ack**; soft bounce and `email.failed` warn and ack. They used to throw → 503 → retry, which could never help two of the three unmatched causes — an account purge deletes the `solicitation_contacts` row `loadAttribution` joins, and a send whose terminal CAS lost never persisted a `provider_message_id` at all. **Exactly one entry in `to`, or throw** — `readRecipients` returns entries UNFILTERED so a non-string member is counted, not silently dropped into a false "exactly one". `reportUnattributedSuppression` raises a Sentry warning post-commit, because acking removes these events from `checkFailedInboundEvents`' `status='failed'` set and a log line replaces no pager. Two rows on one `provider_message_id` throws rather than mutating an arbitrary one. `email.suppressed` emits the loud §6.2 divergence warning **only when a ledger row was really written** — a first-write-wins no-op means the two lists agree, the one case where the divergence claim is false. Logs the bounce **kind**, never the raw provider string (it can carry an address); the full value lands in `provider_error`, a column, not a log. Takes `sql` as a parameter (invariant #15); the attribution `SELECT` is cross-account by construction and allowlisted in the tenant-scope audit. |
