docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:121:- T3 → `purge-verification.test.ts` still gates *arm existence* (`has explicit verification SQL for every disposition table`); only the two-branch behaviour and row survival moved, which a `vi.hoisted` fake tag genuinely cannot assert.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:128:- The retention tests are mutation-verified (documented in the deviations file and consistent with what the assertions would do): dropping `scope = 'account'` from the purge DELETE, or adding `AND suppression.source IS NULL` to `loadCandidateWindow`, each fails a named test.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:134:**One asymmetry worth noting** (not a finding): `batch-builder.integration.test.ts` seeds through the real `recordSuppression` — deliberately, so a fixture cannot carry a pairing the writer can never emit — while `offboarding-lifecycle.integration.test.ts:219` uses a raw `INSERT`. Both choices are defensible (the lifecycle suite needs to seed rows the writer would refuse, to prove the verifier catches them) and the lifecycle suite says so in a comment.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:145:| 2. `retained_suppression_count` optional with reader defaulting to 0 | **Concur with ACCEPT.** This is a genuine rollout invariant, not a speculative shim: `purge_receipt` outbox rows enqueued before the deploy are in flight and must still render, and the same file already carries `recipient_email?` / `purged_at?` optional for the identical reason. It is also *behaviourally correct* — a legacy row renders no sentence rather than asserting "0 retained". Consistency with the two sibling fields is what tips this. One residue in appendix A6. |
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:184:**Concrete failure scenario.** #630 embeds the fragment against a handle carrying stray whitespace or mixed case (`" Alice@Example.com"`). The equality misses, the dispatch gate reports *not suppressed*, and the send proceeds to someone who opted out. Nothing throws, nothing logs, and the `solicitation_suppressed_at_dispatch` event that exists to record the refusal is never emitted — so the failure is invisible in analytics too.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:199:**`worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:757-771`** · **medium**
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:203:> *"a suppression writer MUST NOT write a `scope='account'` row for an account whose `account_offboardings.status` is any ACTIVE teardown state — `'purge_scheduled'` included … A global row written after the boundary MUST carry `accountId: null`… Unreachable today (`recordSuppression` has zero production callers, G6); #644/#645/#627 own honouring it when they land the first ones."*
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:205:The comment is right about the hazard and admirably explicit about *why* no third advisory lock is used (ABBA avoidance). But it is placed in a purge-worker file. The people who need it are the authors of `#644` (redemption), `#645` (manual paths + Correction path) and `#627` (provider webhooks), who will be working in `suppression.ts` and in ingress handlers. Nothing routes them here: no guard in `recordSuppression`, no note on `RecordSuppressionInput`, no test, no CI check, no runbook entry, no ticket-body carry-over that I can verify from this branch.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:209:1. *Self-healing but noisy* — `#645`'s owner-manual path writes a `scope='account'` row while the account sits in `purge_scheduled`. `verifyAccountOffboardingPurge` counts it (branch (a) of the new arm), `finalizePurgeInTransaction` throws `account_offboarding_purge_verification_failed:solicitation_suppressions`, and the purge job fails. Because `handleAccountOffboardingPurge` re-runs `runContentPurgeStage` before `finalizePurgeInTransaction` on retry, the DELETE sweeps the row and the next attempt succeeds — so this one degrades to a spurious failure + alert rather than a wedge.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:210:2. **Not self-healing** — `#645`'s admin path writes a `scope='global'`, `source='admin_manual'` row whose `created_by_user_id` is one of the purging account's users. G14's own comment concedes the type "CANNOT establish that [the actor] is an admin rather than an owner", which is precisely why branch (b) of the verifier is live. But the content-purge stage **never deletes global rows** — that is the whole retention design. So the violation persists across every retry: the account's purge fails verification forever, `account_offboardings.status` never reaches `'purged'`, and the only remediation is a manual DB edit or the not-yet-built Correction path. Neither is documented anywhere in this diff.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:215:- Move the contract text onto `RecordSuppressionInput` / `recordSuppression`'s doc comment in `suppression.ts` — where the three consuming tickets will actually be typing — leaving a one-line pointer in `purge-stages.ts`. Near-zero cost and fixes the audience problem.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:216:- Better: enforce it in `recordSuppression`. It already holds the tx; a guard that refuses a `scope='account'` write when the account has an `account_offboardings` row in an active teardown state is a handful of lines and one test, and it converts a prose MUST into a typed terminal error.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:217:- Separately, add a one-paragraph remediation note for scenario 2 to `docs/runbooks/owner-offboarding-staging-dry-run.md` (or the offboarding spec): an unrecoverable verification failure needs a documented operator path, not a discovery-at-3am path.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:222:`events.md` states `- **Distinct ID:** account ID` for `solicitation_suppressed`, matching the format of the other 22 entries (which use "account ID", "anonymous ID", "anonymous ID or account ID", or "SHA-256 pseudonymous offboarding key" — i.e. the file *does* spell out non-account identities where they exist). The code is:
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:234:**Remedy.** Decide which is true and make the other match. Either document the fallback (`account ID, or the suppression row id when no originating account is known`) — matching how `offboarding_*` documents its pseudonymous key — or give the accountless case a stable non-person identity rather than a per-row UUID.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:248:**`worker/jobs/account-offboarding/__tests__/offboarding-lifecycle.integration.test.ts`** · **low**
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:250:+635 lines, crossing the 1,000-line boundary. The file now carries the offboarding lifecycle, the suppression retention proofs, the token-provenance scrub, purge-verification's two new arms, and the rendered purge-receipt copy assertion — five concerns in one suite.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:267:| No spaghetti growth from special-case branching | **Met.** Both purge additions are straight-line statements inside an existing transaction; neither adds a branch to an existing flow. `toCandidate`'s one new ternary replaces a hardcoded `null` and is the whole point of the seam. |
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:281:**A2. `scope = 'account'` / `scope = 'global'` as bare SQL literals outside the owning module** — `purge-stages.ts:329`, `:775`, `purge-verification.ts:641`, `:644`. The glossary's "never string literals at call sites" sentence is scoped to the seven *sources*, and interpolating `${SUPPRESSION_SCOPES[1]}` into SQL would be strictly less readable than the literal. Arguable either way; not worth a table row.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:289:**A6. `retained_suppression_count?` has no removal trigger.** The optionality is justified by the in-flight rollout window, which closes within minutes of the deploy (purge finalize enqueues, OutboxPoller dispatches). After that the `?` and the `?? 0` are permanently indistinguishable from a legitimate zero. `production.md` warns about "'temporary' branching that is likely to become permanent debt." Given the two sibling fields (`recipient_email?`, `purged_at?`) already have the same shape, tightening this one alone would be inconsistent — so this is really a note about the payload family, not this field.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:295:**A9. Three copies of one privacy assertion.** "A count, never a handle" / "An aggregate only — never handles" / "never lists the handles those rows hold" appear in `outbox.ts:49`, `worker-types.ts:278`, and `account-offboarding.ts:19`. All three are correct and each is at a boundary a reader might arrive at independently, so the redundancy is defensible; but it is three places to update if the field ever changes meaning.
docs/reviews/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core/step7-standards-maintainability.md:297:**A10. `solicitation_suppressions.account_id` is a NO-ACTION FK to `accounts`.** Correct for the offboarding path, where `accounts` rows are tombstoned rather than deleted. Two production paths *do* hard-delete an `accounts` row — `src/lib/onboarding/admin-onboarding-service.ts:249` (creation rollback) and `scripts/ws8-staging-smoke.ts:269` — and both would throw an FK violation if a global suppression row carried that account as provenance. Blast radius is effectively nil (both fire at account-creation time, long before any solicitation send could exist), and the test suites already discovered and handled the same FK in their teardown ordering. Recording it so a future reader who moves `accounts` to a hard delete knows this edge exists.
