docs/reviews/feat-624-spine-lifecycle-fanout/step7-spec-correctness.md:286:| `acquireSolicitationBatchLock` — **new** export, and `batch-builder.ts` moved the advisory lock from *after* the batch row lock to *before* it | `purge-stages.ts:283`, `batch-purge-concurrency.integration.test.ts`, `offboarding-lifecycle.integration.test.ts` | **The riskiest semantic change in the diff, and it holds.** I verified the key derivation is identical to the deleted inline one (`hashtext('solicitation_batch_' || businessId)`) and that the new call uses `preparation.businessId`, which the batch `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` scopes on (`batch-builder.ts:633`) — so it is the same value the old `batch.business_id` produced, and it is now taken *before* the row exists check rather than after, which is what makes the order global. I also checked for a fresh ABBA against `job_outbox`: `constructBatch` takes a `job_outbox` row lock (the ownership CAS) *before* the advisory, so a holder of that advisory must not wait on a `job_outbox` row. `runContentPurgeStage` — the only advisory holder on the purge side — touches no `job_outbox` row; `runRawPayloadPurgeStage` does, but in a separate transaction that takes no advisory lock. No cycle. |
docs/reviews/feat-624-spine-lifecycle-fanout/step7-spec-correctness.md:287:| `runContentPurgeStage` — now acquires a second advisory key per business | `PurgeWorker.ts`, two integration suites | Safe. Both keys are taken in one pass in a fixed order (`csv_ingest` then `solicitation_batch`) over businesses ordered by id, so two concurrent purges cannot invert them. Waiting is the intended behaviour and is bounded by the builder's transaction. |
