docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:129:3. **Agent kinds are pure declarations** (step graph + trigger predicate). Adding an *independent* agent in V2 = drop in `kinds/<name>/`, register any new tools, done. **This drop-in claim holds only for agents that don't coordinate with other agents.** Coordinated agents (e.g. V2 Complaint-Recovery must suppress Reply on the same review) use the **coordination-key primitive shipped in V1** (§3.1 `agent_runs.coordination_key`) — every agent that handles the coordinated subject claims the same subject key at run start, and mutual exclusion falls out of a partial unique index, not new substrate code. We design the *primitive* in V1 precisely so the one feature that would otherwise reopen the substrate is already there.
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:137:7. **Parent/child runs** are first-class via `agent_runs.parent_run_id`. Required by Solicitation Batch → Outreach fan-out; reusable by every future fan-out agent.
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:142:- No generic key/value "memory" store (run state lives on `agent_runs` + domain tables).
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:157:| `agent_runs` | First-class agent run entity. Core observability surface. One row per agent invocation. | `id`, `account_id`, `business_id`, `agent_kind`, `parent_run_id` (nullable), `coordination_key` (nullable text — see below), `status` (`pending`/`running`/`held_for_approval`/`completed`/`failed`/`cancelled`), `trigger_event_id`, `started_at`, `ended_at`, `outcome_summary jsonb` |
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:199:- `agent_runs`: partial unique on `(business_id, coordination_key)` `WHERE coordination_key IS NOT NULL AND status IN ('pending','running','held_for_approval')` — enforces at-most-one active run per coordinated subject. Inert in V1 (key always null); load-bearing for V2 cross-agent suppression.
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:209:| 042 | Substrate core (`agent_kinds`, `agent_runs`, `agent_run_steps`) |
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:348:| Weekly value report + monthly impact email | Read `responses` + `agent_runs`; nothing breaks |
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:649:Every rollback script includes a preflight that checks active `agent_runs`, pending `job_outbox` rows, unconfirmed `outcome_meter_events`, and already-performed tool idempotency keys. If any check is red, the script refuses the bulk rollback and requires per-account operator review.
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:831:10. Parent/child agent runs supported from V1 via `agent_runs.parent_run_id`.
docs/future-plans/2026-05-22-saas-2-0-vertical-agent-platform-design.md:834:12a. Cross-agent coordination primitive (`agent_runs.coordination_key` + partial unique index, §3.1/§3.4) ships in V1's migration 042 but is inert until V2 Complaint-Recovery consumes it. Coordinated agents must claim the same subject key at run start; read-only conflict checks are not sufficient. The "drop-in new agent, no substrate change" claim (principle #3) holds for independent agents; coordinated agents use this primitive.
docs/reviews/feat-726-legacy-hold-gates/standards.md:29:- **No new abstraction, no interface widening.** `src/lib/moderation/held-draft.ts` is untouched by the branch. All three tx call sites (`draft/route.ts:153`, `draft/[responseId]/route.ts:164` and `:245`) are one-line `hasOpenFlag(tx, …)` gates followed by the two-line refusal — the same shape as the pre-existing publish gate (`publish/route.ts:159`). This is the correct "use the canonical helper" move; there is no cleaner code-judo reframing available, because the gates *are* the minimal expression of the plan's locked behavior table.
docs/reviews/feat-726-legacy-hold-gates/standards.md:33:- **Atomicity**: the new `FOR UPDATE` on the POST existing-draft SELECT (D1a) closes the check-then-act gap between the existing-draft read and the held gate; the gate and refusal live inside the same `sql.begin` as the lock. Correct direction — more atomic, not less.
docs/reviews/feat-726-legacy-hold-gates/standards.md:47:- No abstraction created for a single caller; no wrapper around the refusal response (the inline two-line refusal matches the publish precedent and keeps failure mapping in the route layer per invariant #12).
docs/reviews/feat-726-legacy-hold-gates/standards.md:55:- **Queue-mock reconciliation is minimal and correct.** Each pre-existing test that now crosses the gate gains exactly one `[]` rowset (hasOpenFlag → not held) plus an updated tx-queue comment; the DELETE offboarding test's call-count assertion moves 2→3 to match. Nothing else touched. I traced the tx queues against the route control flow (including the text-routed vs queue-consumed offboarding lookup difference between top-level and in-tx mocks) — they reconcile.
docs/reviews/feat-726-legacy-hold-gates/standards.md:56:- **No weakened assertions.** Refusal bodies are asserted **exact** (`toEqual({ error: 'held_for_review' })`), sentinel non-leak checks run against the whole serialized body, and the no-mutation guarantee is asserted structurally (no `UPDATE responses` across all tx calls). The offboarding test kept its `FROM account_offboardings` last-call assertion.
