docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:751:CONTEXT.md:12:- **Drip staleness cap**: the 3-day bound owned by `DRIP_STALENESS_GRACE_DAYS` / `isDripTooStale` in `OnboardingDripWorker` — a drip whose due date passed by more than the grace window is never sent, permanently. It exists so an outage that pauses drips does not clear into a burst of late "Welcome to day 1!" emails. Sends **at** the boundary (the comparison is strictly `<`, so equality is not stale), skips past it. Enforced at **two points, and both are load-bearing**: the `OnboardingDripScheduler` producer excludes stale pairs from the candidate query, and a **fail-closed chokepoint** in `handleOnboardingDrip` re-checks before any side effect. Producer-only was the original design and was wrong — an operator re-driving a failed row, a future second producer, or a row minted just inside the boundary and stranded through a multi-day worker outage all reach the handler with the producer's check long expired; #679 reached the same conclusion for **Import consent** and for the same reason. The chokepoint runs **before** the `onboarding_drip_log` claim (a refusal must not consume the claim a legitimate re-send would need) and **completes** the row carrying `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` rather than failing it — a policy refusal is the guard working, not an incident, and the marker makes a refusal separable in SQL from a genuine send. Unlike #679's guard it needs no `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`: staleness is a pure function of `subscription_activated_at` and the clock, and nothing mutates the anchor, so there is no race to serialize against. Both enforcement points leave a **durable record** since #738: the handler chokepoint completes its outbox row with `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` (7-day window under ArchivalPoller's completed-row retention), and the scheduler stamps `skipped_at`/`skip_reason` on `onboarding_drip_log` (migration 104) — a table ArchivalPoller never touches, so the scheduler's record is the **permanent** answer to "which drips were dropped as too-late, and when". The stamp doubles as the candidate-query dedup, replacing the old `GRACE + 1` one-day reporting window: a too-late pair — including a backlog already past the window at deploy, which the old window skipped silently — is selected once, recorded (a guarded `ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE` stamps a dead claim row; a sent or already-skipped row is untouched, and migration 104's CHECK makes skip-and-send unrepresentable), and excluded thereafter. Removing the outer window is re-mint-safe because archival (7 days) strictly exceeds grace (3 days): any pair archival frees re-enters the query already too late, landing in the skip branch, never the queue branch — that arithmetic is load-bearing; do not shrink outbox archival below the grace window. `skip_reason` vocabulary: `DRIP_SKIP_REASONS` in `OnboardingDripWorker.ts`, derived from `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.DRIP_TOO_STALE` so one grep finds both enforcement points. *(coined in #719; producer-only enforcement corrected at #719 step-7 review A1; observability asymmetry accepted at review A3, then closed by #738/migration 104)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:760:CONTEXT.md:67:- **Body redaction**: the 90-day post-send NULLing of `rendered_subject`/`rendered_body` **and emptying of `interpolation_inputs` to `'{}'`**, with `body_redacted_at` stamped and the metadata row kept forever — data minimization on third-party PII held on the contact's behalf, timed comfortably past the 14-day attribution window and past bounce/complaint feedback lag. The source goes with the prose: `interpolation_inputs` carries `contact_first_name` and `visit_note`, so redacting only the rendered text would delete the sentence and keep its ingredients. This is enforced in the schema, not merely in the sweep — `solicitation_outreach_body_presence` requires `body_redacted_at IS NULL OR interpolation_inputs = '{}'::jsonb`, so no future writer can stamp a redaction and skip the erasure. It costs the post-90-day reproducibility of the `unverifiable_fact` provenance verdict (#623 §2.4), which was already lost at the same instant anyway: that verdict is computed over `subject + body`, both of which this same sweep deletes. It is **not a delete**, which is why the retention inventory carries it as a novel shape (redact-in-place, plus purge-by-cascade). **Redaction implies TERMINAL, and that is one window on two clocks**: a `sent` row ages from `sent_at`, while a row that settled without ever sending (`withheld`/`cancelled`/`failed`/`unknown`) has no `sent_at` and ages from `rendered_at`. A LIVE row (`rendered`/`queued`/`sending`) is never redactable at any age — a bodyless live attempt can neither send nor settle, and wedges its batch behind `solicitation_outreach_one_live_per_ask`, which is the hazard the rule exists for. #624 shipped the rule as "implies **sent**", which made the stamp unrepresentable on the four never-sent terminals and left their bodies and inputs retained forever (#636 / step-7 S2); 092 widened it to the hazard's actual shape. Swept by two bounded arms of the existing `ArchivalPoller` — no new poller, because the single-replica P2 duplicate-processing budget was already spent — each oldest-first with `SKIP LOCKED`, capped at 5000 rows per cycle and logged as saturated at its own cap. A body is never logged. _Avoid_: body purge, body expiry. *(coined in #619 D6b/D6c; swept in #624; completed in #636)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:761:CONTEXT.md:69:- **Attested postal address**: the owner-attested `businesses.solicitation_postal_address` plus its attestation quartet (`_attested_at`, `_attested_by_user_id`, `_attestation_version`, `_attested_digest`) — the CAN-SPAM §7704(a)(5) sender address baked **verbatim** into every ask footer, and a send precondition. Deliberately **not** `google_place_address`, which is Google's `formattedAddress`: it can be a service-area value like `"Austin, TX, USA"`, is not owner-attested, cannot express a USPS-registered PO box, and is NULLed by the offboarding purge (spec §7.5). Address and attestation are **all-or-nothing by DB CHECK**, and the attestation is welded to the address's **current bytes** by a second CHECK over `_attested_digest` — so no code path can read an unattested address as attested, and no writer can change the stored bytes without recomputing that digest. That guarantee is **narrower than "the attestation is always current"**, and the narrow statement is the load-bearing one: the digest CHECK proves the digest matches the CURRENT address, not that the preserved `_attested_at` / `_attested_by_user_id` / `_attestation_version` ever covered those bytes — a writer that changes the address AND recomputes the digest while keeping the old metadata still passes. Closing that residual needs `OLD` vs `NEW`, which a CHECK cannot see; the trigger was declined by owner decision (2026-08-04, plan "Design decisions" #1). The accidental path — a future `UPDATE … SET solicitation_postal_address = $1` that forgets the attestation — is closed; the residual is forgery rather than oversight. `_attested_by_user_id` is a **plain UUID with no FK** — a point-in-time capture, per migration 093's written lesson that 086's `ON DELETE CASCADE` meant deleting a staff member deleted their uploads. Because there is no FK, the *writer* verifies the actor. _Avoid_: business address, mailing address, footer address. *(coined in #656 — migration 099; owned by `src/lib/solicitation/postal-address.ts`)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:762:CONTEXT.md:74:- **Restraint policy**: the deterministic, sentiment-blind rule set that decides send vs withhold for each Solicitation candidate — the refusal logic is the product; everything else displays its decisions. No LLM in the decision path. *(coined in the §9 solicitation roadmap; locked in ticket #533)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:763:CONTEXT.md:75:- **Withhold reason**: one code from the closed six-reason enum (`no_consent`, `suppressed`, `already_reviewed`, `stale_visit`, `over_contacted`, `velocity_banked`) explaining why a candidate was not asked. Every reason is sentiment-blind by construction; `recent_negative` is permanently rejected (ADR 0012). First-match precedence; one reason per withhold. _Avoid_: refusal code, skip reason. *(locked in ticket #533)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:769:CONTEXT.md:95:- **Consent ledger**: the `solicitation_consent` table (migration 093, #625) — one current-state row per `(channel, normalized_handle, business_id)` answering "which attestation covers this handle, for this channel, at this location." Written ONLY by csv ingest inside the per-business advisory-locked transaction (D8), and only for the resolved contact's RETAINED email (§7.1 deletability — `consentEligibleEmail`); phone handles mint zero rows (DR3, email-only). **Monotone forward** (§4.2): the guarded upsert (`WHERE EXCLUDED.attested_at > attested_at`) upgrades to a newer attestation and never downgrades, so replayed jobs are no-ops and an in-flight pre-deploy v1 job cannot overwrite a v2 row; a version change emits `solicitation_consent_upgraded`, a same-version re-attestation bumps silently. Send-eligibility is `attestation_version ∈ SEND_ELIGIBLE_ATTESTATION_VERSIONS` (v2-only — v1's text never named email sending, which is the `consent_attested` tautology #625 killed); an ineligible-version row withholds `no_consent` **case (b)** (upload_id + attestation_version populated), no row at all is **case (a)** (both null — the whole pre-#625 legacy cohort, no backfill by decision; #629's case-(a) copy must be self-sufficient). Revocation lives in the suppression ledger, never here (D7 — no `revoked_at`, ever): consent fails closed, suppression fails open (DR1), so consent rows hard-delete with their contact (by handle, same tx) and via an explicit purge-stage sweep (spec §7.2 as corrected — businesses are tombstoned, the cascade never fires on purge). RLS deny-all; no TTL (§7.3). _Avoid_: consent flag, attested boolean, consent snapshot (that is what #630 stamps on outreach at dispatch). *(coined in #620 grilling; shipped by #625, PR #640)*
