touches, so the scheduler's record is the **permanent** answer to "which drips were dropped as too-late, and when". The stamp doubles as the candidate-query dedup, replacing the old `GRACE + 1` one-day reporting window: a too-late pair — including a backlog already past the window at deploy, which the old window skipped silently — is selected once, recorded (a guarded `ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE` stamps a dead claim row; a sent or already-skipped row is untouched, and migration 104's CHECK makes skip-and-send unrepresentable), and excluded thereafter. Removing the outer window is re-mint-safe because archival (7 days) strictly exceeds grace (3 days): any pair archival frees re-enters the query already too late, landing in the skip branch, never the queue branch — that arithmetic is load-bearing; do not shrink outbox archival below the grace window. `skip_reason` vocabulary: `DRIP_SKIP_REASONS` in `OnboardingDripWorker.ts`, derived from `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.DRIP_TOO_STALE` so one grep finds both enforcement points. *(coined in #719; producer-only enforcement corrected at #719 step-7 review A1; observability asymmetry accepted at review A3, then closed by #738/migration 104)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:1921:    28	- **Import consent**: the per-location decision record for the Review import offer — `import_decision` (`pending` | `accepted` | `declined`), `import_decision_at`, `import_offer_expires_at`, columns on `businesses` (migration 100, #673). **Expiry is derived, never stored** (#670): "expired" = `pending AND import_offer_expires_at <= now()`, computed on read by one predicate helper — no poller, no `expired` enum value, and nothing fires at the expiry moment. The single owner of the predicate and of every write is `src/lib/reviews/import-consent.ts` (#677): `importConsentStateSql` composes the derivation into a larger query (**callers must alias `businesses` as `b`**), `readImportConsent` returns the row, `createImportOffer` stamps the deadline at the junction, and `recordImportDecisionAndEnqueue` owns the acceptance transaction. Enforcement today is **at the four product enqueue paths** (#677) — connect finalize, the Stripe activation resume, reactivation catch-up, and the owner's decision route — each gating on `accepted` while holding the `businesses` row lock. The **fail-closed worker guard shipped at #679** (PR #698): `guardImportConsent` (`worker/jobs/zernio/job-eligibility.ts`) runs in `ZernioBackfillWorker` before `guardZernioJobEligibility` and before the run-row INSERT, and proceeds only on `accepted` — so declined, derived-expired, pending and a missing `businesses` row, including hand-inserted rows i.e. the admin path, all no-op-complete with a logged `reason`. Since #695 a refused row is also **marked for the archival window** — `job_outbox.completion_reason = 'import_consent_blocked'` (the `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON` vocabulary, column from migration 083) — so a policy refusal is separable by SQL from a genuine completed import, which before was only inferable from the log line plus the absence of a `zernio_backfill_runs` row. **It is an operational-window signal, NOT an audit record**: `ArchivalPoller` deletes completed `job_outbox` rows 7 days after `completed_at`, and a refusal completes rather than fails, so it takes the 7-day arm — the same life `owner_unreachable` has always had (ADR 0010), a property of the sink rather than of #695. What outlives the window is `import_decision` / `import_decision_at` on `businesses`, plus the log line under the log sink's own retention. ONE marker for all four refusal states: it records THAT a refusal happened, and the state that caused it lives only in the log line — a later read of `businesses` can disagree (a `pending` refusal the owner then accepts) or find nothing (the `missing` case). Nothing reads it in code: no poller, no alert, no admin surface — a refusal is the guard working. No backfill, so a NULL on a row predating #695 is not evidence of a genuine import. The `job_outbox`-boundary alternative was weighed and declined at #695 (ADR 0016, `docs/adr/0016-import-consent-enforcement-locus.md`). D8's "admin path included" now holds at the one chokepoint every enqueue path shares. It is deliberately a SEPARATE function, not a `jobKind` branch: a merged guard would have two callers, one of which must never reach consent code, so splitting makes live-ingest independence structural rather than a runtime branch. Its whole body runs inside `sql.begin` with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` + `markOutboxCompletedWithReason(tx, …)`, and both halves are load-bearing — without them the guard can read `pending` while the owner accepts, the acceptance's in-flight dedup inserts no replacement, and the guard completes the row: `accepted` with no completed run and no runnable outbox row, i.e. silent permanent loss for a customer who said yes. Accepted-but-never-completed stays retryable via the `completed_at IS NULL` sentinel (spec §5.1). Distinct from the solicitation **Consent attestation** / **Consent ledger** below — different domain, different tables, no shared machinery. _Avoid_: bare "consent" (ambiguous with solicitation), backfill consent. *(coined in #670; spec §5.3; worker-guard wording corrected from built to pending at #677 — round-1 cited-artifact check — then to shipped at #679, PR #698; durable block reason + enforcement-locus ADR at #695)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:1956:    63	- **Send batch parent**: the `agent_runs` row of kind `solicitation_send_batch` (`subject_type='solicitation_batch'`) — the sequential sibling of watch-mode's terminal `solicitation_batch` build run. It starts when the send lane engages (generation), spans the approval wait, and terminalizes when the drip closes. Legitimately long-lived but bounded **by construction** at ≤10 days (7-day approval window + 72-hour send window), which is exactly why the spine's `stalled` threshold became kind-aware rather than raised by feel. _Avoid_: send run, drip run. *(coined in #619 D3; ADR 0014)*
docs/reviews/feat-757-dashboard-review-time-anchor/adversarial-approach-round1.log:1958:    65	- **Settled batch**: a batch with no outreach row left in `rendered`, `queued` or `sending` — the join condition, asked of the domain table and never of `agent_runs`. Every writer that terminalizes an **Outreach row** calls `closeBatchIfSettled` in the *same* transaction, so there is no window between terminalizing and joining; it CASes `send_closed_at` on NULL (a second caller writes nothing, not even a duplicate receipt), records one close step, and terminalizes the **Send batch parent** through the disposition lookup (`exhausted`→`completed`; `halted`/`expired`→`cancelled`; `gate_closed`/`generation_failed`→`failed`). The releaser tick is a backstop calling the same function — one implementation, two callers, no join poller. Disposition is an ordered precedence over explicit sources; the status counts are the receipt's *evidence*, not the decision. *(coined in #619 D11/D14; `closeBatchIfSettled` in `src/lib/solicitation/outreach.ts`)*
