docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:5:**Goal:** Ship the **Suppression ledger**'s schema and domain core — `solicitation_suppressions` and `solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` (migrations 096/097), the single authoritative `src/lib/solicitation/suppression.ts`, the build-time populate seam, offboarding purge retention, and the analytics registrations that #644, #645, #627 and #630 all build on.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:7:**Architecture:** One new deep module (`suppression.ts`) exporting three `as const` enum arrays, one **composable SQL fragment** that is the sole owner of the §1.2 lookup predicate, and one first-write-wins writer. Every other change deepens an existing module without widening its interface — the sole exception is the `purge_receipt` outbox payload, which gains one integer. All persistence flows through explicit `sql`/`tx` parameters (invariant #15).
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:28:| G12 | Token provenance obeys a **one-way implication**: `account_id IS NULL` implies `business_id` and `ask_id` are null | **Added round 1 (finding 6), corrected round 2 (finding 14) and round 3 (finding 10).** NOT a symmetric all-or-none — that form would force `ask_id NOT NULL` on every live row, over-constraining an optional provenance column. The implication is exactly what purge verification needs: scrubbing `account_id` guarantees the rest are scrubbed, so checking `account_id`/`business_id` proves `ask_id` went too. Deliberately **excludes** `outreach_id`, which SET NULL may null independently while the account is still live. |
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:29:| G14 | The global input variant splits into **`admin_manual`** (actor + note required) and **machine sources** (`createdByUserId?: never`) | **Added round 2 (finding 16), scoped round 3 (finding 11).** §7.3 requires an actor and note for admin entry. G14 enforces **field presence per source**, nothing more — it types the actor as a plain string, so it cannot prove the actor is an admin. Establishing admin identity is #645's caller-side authorization concern. This is why the purge verifier's global owner-actor branch stays live: it is the detector for a caller that got that wrong. |
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:32:| G4 | 096 ships **`account_id`-leading indexes** on both tables | Both partial uniques lead with `(channel, normalized_handle)`, but the purge delete, retained count and token scrub all filter account-first. PG16 has no skip scan. This is migration 095's lesson applied up front. |
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:33:| G5 | Retained-count is **global suppression rows only**, snapshotted at purge time and threaded through the outbox payload | Token rows are opt-out *mechanisms*, not opt-out *records*, and their `account_id` is scrubbed by then. Threading (vs. querying at send) matches the existing row-less `purge_receipt` posture, keeps the email worker ignorant of the solicitation domain, and makes a retried send render a stable number. |
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-01-suppression-ledger-core.md:37:| G9 | `VerifyAccountOffboardingPurgeInput` is **NOT** widened | The account's users are derivable by subquery, exactly as the `analytics_events` arm already does at `purge-verification.ts:351`. |
