docs/superpowers/plans/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core-intake.json:121:  "rollback_plan": "Ordered, and the window closes when the first writer ships. (1) 097 is a strict CHECK superset of 094's list — reverting the deploy leaves the constraint valid and all existing rows passing, exactly as 094's own rollback note states; no down-migration needed. (2) 096 creates two tables with ZERO production writers in this ticket, so while they are empty `DROP TABLE solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens; DROP TABLE solicitation_suppressions;` is safe. Once #644/#645/#627 land writers it is NOT safe — dropping would destroy compliance records — so this rollback is valid only before the first writer merges. (3) Code revert MUST precede any table drop: batch-builder's LATERAL join, the purge-verification arms and the purge-stages scrub all reference the tables, and dropping first turns every batch build and every offboarding purge into a hard error. Revert the app deploy, confirm no traffic, then drop. (4) Migrations apply staging-first and prod at promotion, in lockstep.",
docs/superpowers/plans/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core-intake.json:123:    "Sentry: `account_offboarding_purge_verification_failed:solicitation_suppressions` — fires when an account-scoped row survived the purge, or when a retained global row still names one of the account's users. Its ABSENCE does NOT prove global rows were retained: the verifier counts violations, so a purge that wrongly deleted every global row also reports zero. Retention is proven by the live-DB survival test, never by this signal (corrected in adversarial review round 2, finding 18).",
docs/superpowers/plans/feat-643-suppression-ledger-core-intake.json:124:    "Sentry: `missing_purge_verification_sql:solicitation_suppressions` or `:solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` — fires if the disposition entry lands without its verification arm (the fail-closed guard).",
