docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-656-postal-address-attestation.md:7:**Architecture:** One new domain module (`src/lib/solicitation/postal-address.ts`) owns the attestation-text version, the light lint, the atomic attestation write, the read model, and the withheld-reason rule. Migration 099 adds **five** nullable columns to `businesses` under **two** CHECKs — one all-or-nothing over the tuple, one binding the attestation to the *current bytes* via a stored SHA-256 digest — so no caller ever branches on "is this attested" and an address-only UPDATE by any writer fails closed. Everything else is a thin edit at an existing seam: the settings page server-fetches and passes props, the batch builder widens a `SELECT` it already runs, the worker adds one log field, the purge adds five columns to a `SET` it already writes, and ask-generation **deletes** its `to_jsonb` shim.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-656-postal-address-attestation.md:34:- **Attested postal address** — the owner-attested `businesses.solicitation_postal_address` plus its attestation quartet (`_attested_at`, `_attested_by_user_id`, `_attestation_version`, `_attested_digest`): the CAN-SPAM §7704(a)(5) sender address baked **verbatim** into every ask footer, and a send precondition. Deliberately **not** `google_place_address`, which is Google's `formattedAddress` — it can be a service-area value like `"Austin, TX, USA"`, is not owner-attested, cannot express a USPS-registered PO box, and is NULLed by the offboarding purge (spec §7.5). Address and attestation are **all-or-nothing by DB CHECK**, and the attestation is welded to the address's **current bytes** by a second CHECK over `_attested_digest` — so no code path can read an unattested address as attested, and no writer can change the stored bytes without recomputing that digest (see "Design decisions" #1 for the exact scope of that guarantee — it is narrower than "the attestation is always current"). `_attested_by_user_id` is a **plain UUID with no FK** — a point-in-time capture, per migration 093's written lesson that 086's `ON DELETE CASCADE` meant deleting a staff member deleted their uploads. Because there is no FK, the *writer* verifies the actor. _Avoid_: business address, mailing address, footer address.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-656-postal-address-attestation.md:49:Verified in `src/lib/auth/route-helpers.ts:221-238`: `requireSettingsOwner` resolves **no location** and — per its own doc comment at :217-220 — **deliberately skips the writable-subscription gate** ("settings reads and rival/auto-approval writes stay available to past-due accounts"). The intake demands both a cookie-resolved active location *and* a non-writable-subscription refusal, and `requireSettingsOwner` supplies neither.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-656-postal-address-attestation.md:56:- The operational-UI offboarding block. **NOT safe to lose — the route must re-add it explicitly.** Round 2 disproved the earlier claim that the subscription gate covers it: `isFullBlockState` returns true for `mode === 'delete_data'` at *any* status (`src/lib/account-offboarding/gate.ts:212-215`), and confirming offboarding does not itself cancel the subscription — Stripe is the source of truth and cancellation is a separate async step. So an account in `delete_data/pending` is still `active`, sails through `assertSubscriptionAllowsOwnerScope`, and would be able to mutate its sender address mid-teardown.
docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-656-postal-address-attestation.md:60:> **T4 differs from it in ORDERING, deliberately.** The draft route runs its check at `:103-106` — *after* the body parse (`:89-96`) and after `authorizeEditReplyResponse` (`:99`). T4 runs it **before the body parse**, which is stricter: a blocked account gets `403 account_offboarding_blocked` even for a malformed body, rather than a 400 that leaks "your body was the problem" to an account that may not write at all. Do not read "same as the draft route" as "same position" — copy the mechanism, keep T4's order. *(Corrected after T4 found the two readings diverge.)*
