CONTEXT.md:4:- **Response / draft**: the LLM-generated reply to a review; it is only a draft until an approval method is recorded. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
CONTEXT.md:6:- **Moderation hold**: a `high`-severity moderation flag writes a `flagged_responses` row (status `open`) and suppresses owner notification. A draft is **held** while its flag is `open`; the single read-side spelling of that predicate is `openFlagExistsSql` (`src/lib/moderation/held-draft.ts`, #730 — its header also inventories the deliberately distinct consumers that do NOT consolidate onto it). Any disposition off `open` (admin `approved_for_owner_review`, `dismissed_false_positive`, `requires_regeneration`) closes held-ness; only `approved_for_owner_review` additionally re-notifies the owner. *(draft — sharpened at #730 review; was: "only admin approval releases it", which conflated flag-release with re-notification)*
CONTEXT.md:8:- **Outbox job**: follow-up work recorded in `job_outbox` in the same transaction as the state change; OutboxPoller dispatches it to BullMQ. *(draft — to be sharpened in grilling sessions)*
CONTEXT.md:12:- **Drip staleness cap**: the 3-day bound owned by `DRIP_STALENESS_GRACE_DAYS` / `isDripTooStale` in `OnboardingDripWorker` — a drip whose due date passed by more than the grace window is never sent, permanently. It exists so an outage that pauses drips does not clear into a burst of late "Welcome to day 1!" emails. Sends **at** the boundary (the comparison is strictly `<`, so equality is not stale), skips past it. Enforced at **two points, and both are load-bearing**: the `OnboardingDripScheduler` producer excludes stale pairs from the candidate query, and a **fail-closed chokepoint** in `handleOnboardingDrip` re-checks before any side effect. Producer-only was the original design and was wrong — an operator re-driving a failed row, a future second producer, or a row minted just inside the boundary and stranded through a multi-day worker outage all reach the handler with the producer's check long expired; #679 reached the same conclusion for **Import consent** and for the same reason. The chokepoint runs **before** the `onboarding_drip_log` claim (a refusal must not consume the claim a legitimate re-send would need) and **completes** the row carrying `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` rather than failing it — a policy refusal is the guard working, not an incident, and the marker makes a refusal separable in SQL from a genuine send. Unlike #679's guard it needs no `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`: staleness is a pure function of `subscription_activated_at` and the clock, and nothing mutates the anchor, so there is no race to serialize against. Both enforcement points leave a **durable record** since #738: the handler chokepoint completes its outbox row with `completion_reason = 'drip_too_stale'` (7-day window under ArchivalPoller's completed-row retention), and the scheduler stamps `skipped_at`/`skip_reason` on `onboarding_drip_log` (migration 104) — a table ArchivalPoller never touches, so the scheduler's record is the **permanent** answer to "which drips were dropped as too-late, and when". The stamp doubles as the candidate-query dedup, replacing the old `GRACE + 1` one-day reporting window: a too-late pair — including a backlog already past the window at deploy, which the old window skipped silently — is selected once, recorded (a guarded `ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE` stamps a dead claim row; a sent or already-skipped row is untouched, and migration 104's CHECK makes skip-and-send unrepresentable), and excluded thereafter. Removing the outer window is re-mint-safe because archival (7 days) strictly exceeds grace (3 days): any pair archival frees re-enters the query already too late, landing in the skip branch, never the queue branch — that arithmetic is load-bearing; do not shrink outbox archival below the grace window. `skip_reason` vocabulary: `DRIP_SKIP_REASONS` in `OnboardingDripWorker.ts`, derived from `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON.DRIP_TOO_STALE` so one grep finds both enforcement points. *(coined in #719; producer-only enforcement corrected at #719 step-7 review A1; observability asymmetry accepted at review A3, then closed by #738/migration 104)*
CONTEXT.md:13:- **Job-failure sink**: `worker/job-failure-sink.ts`, attached to every BullMQ `Worker` alongside `attachEmitterErrorLogger` — on the **terminal** attempt only it raises a Sentry issue (tags `{subsystem, queue, topic}`) rather than the breadcrumb that preceded it. Its terminal test is the **`'failed'`-event** predicate `attemptsMade >= max`, deliberately NOT `isTerminalAttempt` from `src/lib/shared/outbox.ts`: that one is `attemptsMade >= max || attemptsMade + 1 >= max`, written for the **catch block** where `attemptsMade` has not yet been incremented, and using it in the event context fired one attempt early *and* again on the real last one — a duplicate issue plus a false "exhausted its retries" for a job BullMQ then retried successfully. `worker/jobs/judge/failed-hook.ts` keeps its own copy for the same reason and says "do not unify them"; treat any future shared helper here as a trap. It writes **nothing** to `job_outbox`: the **Watchdog** overwrites `last_error` unconditionally when it terminalizes, and a raw driver message can embed contact PII, so durable per-domain error text stays the job of the six domain `'failed'` hooks — Sentry is the durable record of the *handler error* specifically, not of every failure fact. Promoting these from breadcrumbs to issues moved the message onto the one Sentry field `scrubSentryEvent` did not cover, so that scrubber now redacts `exception.values` too. *(coined in #719; terminal predicate and exception scrubbing corrected at #719 step-7 review A2/A6)*
CONTEXT.md:23:- **Deployment lane** (the "lane") — which of the three environments a running process is in, in CLAUDE.md's existing "Three lanes" sense (*preview* / *staging* / *production*). Resolved by the single authoritative owner `src/lib/config/deployment-lane.ts` from `RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_NAME` (the Railway-injected system variable — wins whenever present), falling back to `NODE_ENV`, then `'development'`. **A lane is not `NODE_ENV`**: staging runs with `NODE_ENV=production` and production leaves `NODE_ENV` unset, so `NODE_ENV` cannot name the lane (#610/#611). It is also **not a feature flag** — it reports where the process runs and never gates a product behaviour, which is why it lives outside `src/lib/config/flags.ts`. The separate `requiresProductionConfig()` answers "must this process satisfy production-grade config?" and is deliberately the *union* of the two signals, never their precedence. *(coined in the #610/#611 deployment-lane fix; ADR 0013)*
CONTEXT.md:25:- **Owner-unreachable outcome** — the notification result when an approval draft's account/location has zero verified channel enrollments at send time (`no_verified_enrollments`), recorded as `job_outbox.completion_reason='owner_unreachable'` on the completed `send_notification` row, and *current* only while that is the **latest** completed `send_notification` for the response. Distinct from **all-failed** (a delivery was attempted on an enrolled channel and every attempt failed) and **stale** (delivered but the owner hasn't acted). The draft stays `pending_approval` and is retried on the `NotificationFailoverPoller` cadence until a channel appears. *(coined in the F1 owner-unreachable escalation effort)*
CONTEXT.md:28:- **Import consent**: the per-location decision record for the Review import offer — `import_decision` (`pending` | `accepted` | `declined`), `import_decision_at`, `import_offer_expires_at`, columns on `businesses` (migration 100, #673). **Expiry is derived, never stored** (#670): "expired" = `pending AND import_offer_expires_at <= now()`, computed on read by one predicate helper — no poller, no `expired` enum value, and nothing fires at the expiry moment. The single owner of the predicate and of every write is `src/lib/reviews/import-consent.ts` (#677): `importConsentStateSql` composes the derivation into a larger query (**callers must alias `businesses` as `b`**), `readImportConsent` returns the row, `createImportOffer` stamps the deadline at the junction, and `recordImportDecisionAndEnqueue` owns the acceptance transaction. Enforcement today is **at the four product enqueue paths** (#677) — connect finalize, the Stripe activation resume, reactivation catch-up, and the owner's decision route — each gating on `accepted` while holding the `businesses` row lock. The **fail-closed worker guard shipped at #679** (PR #698): `guardImportConsent` (`worker/jobs/zernio/job-eligibility.ts`) runs in `ZernioBackfillWorker` before `guardZernioJobEligibility` and before the run-row INSERT, and proceeds only on `accepted` — so declined, derived-expired, pending and a missing `businesses` row, including hand-inserted rows i.e. the admin path, all no-op-complete with a logged `reason`. Since #695 a refused row is also **marked for the archival window** — `job_outbox.completion_reason = 'import_consent_blocked'` (the `OUTBOX_COMPLETION_REASON` vocabulary, column from migration 083) — so a policy refusal is separable by SQL from a genuine completed import, which before was only inferable from the log line plus the absence of a `zernio_backfill_runs` row. **It is an operational-window signal, NOT an audit record**: `ArchivalPoller` deletes completed `job_outbox` rows 7 days after `completed_at`, and a refusal completes rather than fails, so it takes the 7-day arm — the same life `owner_unreachable` has always had (ADR 0010), a property of the sink rather than of #695. What outlives the window is `import_decision` / `import_decision_at` on `businesses`, plus the log line under the log sink's own retention. ONE marker for all four refusal states: it records THAT a refusal happened, and the state that caused it lives only in the log line — a later read of `businesses` can disagree (a `pending` refusal the owner then accepts) or find nothing (the `missing` case). Nothing reads it in code: no poller, no alert, no admin surface — a refusal is the guard working. No backfill, so a NULL on a row predating #695 is not evidence of a genuine import. The `job_outbox`-boundary alternative was weighed and declined at #695 (ADR 0016, `docs/adr/0016-import-consent-enforcement-locus.md`). D8's "admin path included" now holds at the one chokepoint every enqueue path shares. It is deliberately a SEPARATE function, not a `jobKind` branch: a merged guard would have two callers, one of which must never reach consent code, so splitting makes live-ingest independence structural rather than a runtime branch. Its whole body runs inside `sql.begin` with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` + `markOutboxCompletedWithReason(tx, …)`, and both halves are load-bearing — without them the guard can read `pending` while the owner accepts, the acceptance's in-flight dedup inserts no replacement, and the guard completes the row: `accepted` with no completed run and no runnable outbox row, i.e. silent permanent loss for a customer who said yes. Accepted-but-never-completed stays retryable via the `completed_at IS NULL` sentinel (spec §5.1). Distinct from the solicitation **Consent attestation** / **Consent ledger** below — different domain, different tables, no shared machinery. _Avoid_: bare "consent" (ambiguous with solicitation), backfill consent. *(coined in #670; spec §5.3; worker-guard wording corrected from built to pending at #677 — round-1 cited-artifact check — then to shipped at #679, PR #698; durable block reason + enforcement-locus ADR at #695)*
CONTEXT.md:29:- **Import offer**: the one-time, per-location invitation to run the Review import, created **server-side at the subscribe+connect junction** — activation non-NULL **and** an active `zernio_connections` row, both proven inside `createImportOffer`'s own SQL rather than trusted from callers. Its deadline is `now() + 7 days` **at creation**; there is no junction anchor (owner adjudication 2026-08-06 — anchoring was self-invented, produced two High findings across two review rounds, and was deleted). An offer is **answerable only while it exists and is live**: the guarded transition requires `import_decision = 'pending' AND import_offer_expires_at IS NOT NULL AND > now()`, so migration 100's default `pending` + NULL deadline is deliberately NOT an offer — that `IS NOT NULL` is what stops a direct POST manufacturing consent for a location nothing was ever offered for. Answering is terminal and first-write-wins; a self-healed offer (repaired by the dashboard GET when the junction-time write failed) starts a fresh 7 days at the repair, which is more generous and is the point. Surfaced by `ImportOfferBanner` through `GET/POST /api/dashboard/import-offer`, never from the Redis-cached dashboard payload. **Inheritance on re-add copies a decision, and a deadline only when that deadline is already in the past** (#677 D12): re-adding a location whose archived row `declined` or let the offer expire does not re-offer the import — the past deadline is copied so the new row still derives expired — but a **live** deadline is left NULL, so the new row earns a fresh offer at its OWN junction. Copying a live deadline would present an answerable offer for a location with no active connection, bypassing the very junction proof `createImportOffer` exists to enforce. Distinct from **Import consent**, which is the stored decision record this offer collects. _Avoid_: import prompt, backfill offer, "the banner" (that is the surface, not the offer). *(coined in #677; spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-05-consented-review-import-design.md` §3, §5.3)*
CONTEXT.md:32:- **Reviews list**: the owner's single inventory of every review for a location — the surface the consented Review import lands on. It is **not a new page**: `dashboard/reviews/history` (the former Past Replies archive) is widened in place to include never-replied reviews and becomes it (#671, 2026-08-08; spec §6.3a S1). Grouped `Unanswered` then `Answered` per D6, newest-first inside each group, `PAGE_SIZE` 50. Distinct from the **Needs Action** queue at `dashboard/reviews`, which survives unchanged and answers a different question: that surface filters to `pending_approval` + live auto-post veto windows off the Redis-cached dashboard payload (`ReviewFeedCard.tsx:59-66`, `LIMIT 10`) and can never show an imported review. A review with a pending draft appears in **both** — in the list as a `Draft ready` cross-link with no `Generate` button. Per-row actions split by risk: `Generate` (never-replied) expands the row inline (`InlineGeneratePanel`), `Regenerate` (already-replied) opens the `EditReplyDrawer` two-pane comparison because publishing there **replaces** the owner's live Google reply (spec §6.4). As built (#727), every affordance derives from two orthogonal axes modelled server-side (ADR 0018; replaces #678's six-valued `row_state` precedence chain `recover → regenerate → held → draft_ready → generating → generate`): the **Posted-reply axis** (`reply_state`: `none` / `posted` / `orphan`) — what exists on Google for the review; owns the Unanswered/Answered grouping and the reply panel — and the server-internal **Replacement-draft axis** (`draft_state`: `none` / `generating` / `ready` / `held`, intra-axis precedence held > ready > generating > none), which **never crosses the wire**. The card receives only `reply_state` plus the **Action slot** (`row_action`: `generate` / `generating` / `draft_ready` / `regenerate` / `recover` / `none`) — the single affordance derived from the full 3×4 cross-product (`deriveAxes` + `deriveRowAction`, `src/lib/past-replies/replies-view.ts`). A held draft never projects an affordance of its own (invariant #4 as structure): `none`×held and `posted`×held project `row_action 'none'` — a posted row keeps its live Google reply visible and silently withdraws Regenerate — while `orphan`×held keeps `recover`, because recovery addresses the orphan incident, not the draft. The `held` value itself never reaches the browser: the card reconstructs the "Reply on hold" panel for an unanswered held row from `reply_state 'none'` + `row_action 'none'`. The `gap` / `historical` section split derived from `subscription_activated_at` is deleted here, surviving only as a per-row `ProvenanceBadge` (`posted_before_joining` / `replied_on_google` / `auto_approved` / `owner_approved`); `PAST_REPLIES_VIEW_ENABLED` was **retired with it at #678** — the list renders unconditionally — while `EDIT_REPLIES_ENABLED` is kept (it gates the live-Google mutation) and `editRepliesEnabled()` (`src/lib/past-replies/flags.ts`) is now its only reader. _Avoid_: Past Replies, the archive, History tab (all name the pre-#671 surface), All Reviews page (it is a tab, not a page). *(coined in #671; spec §6.3a, §6.4; implemented at #678; remodelled at #727 per #723)*
CONTEXT.md:33:- **On-demand generation**: owner-initiated LLM drafting from the **Reviews list** — `Generate` on a never-replied review, `Regenerate` on an already-replied one — as opposed to the live-ingest lane, which drafts automatically on every newly ingested review. It is an **owner-controlled LLM spend surface**, governed by four already-existing layers, not by new machinery (#672, 2026-08-08; spec §6.3b): a per-owner-account **rate** limit (`dashregen:owner:{acct}:burst` 10/60s + `:hour` 30/hr, **fails open**), a per-review daily cap (`regen:{reviewId}:{date}`, 3/day — advisory peek at the route, authoritative Lua INCR in `generate-draft.ts:109`, terminal), and the per-business/global **spend** reservation (`cost:daily:{bizId}:{date}`, `cost:global:{date}`). The per-account axis is denominated in **cents, not clicks**. The first `Generate` on a review is **free by construction** — `generate-draft.ts:106` charges only `version > 1`, so the sequence is one free draft then three charged regenerations per review per day; the guard stays because it is what stops the live lane's own auto-draft from eating the owner's regen budget. All three doors share **one** counter, so alternating `Generate` and `Regenerate` cannot double throughput. Because the spend ceiling is shared with the live lane and exhausting it **terminally drops** a draft, `DAILY_COST_LIMIT_CENTS_*` carries a sizing constraint rather than a reserved floor (C4). As built at #678 the two doors are `POST /api/dashboard/reviews/[reviewId]/generate` (never-replied; `origin_lane='on_demand_generate'`, always owner-only — never auto-approved) and the pre-existing `/api/dashboard/past-replies/draft` Regenerate lane, with `GET /api/dashboard/reviews/[reviewId]/draft-status` polling by `reviewId`; a moderation-held response returns `{ status: 'idle' }` and never its text. _Avoid_: on-demand drafting, manual generation, the regenerate budget (that names one layer, not the policy). *(coined in #672; spec §6.3b; implemented at #678)*
CONTEXT.md:35:- **Channel actor**: the resolved identity a mobile-channel enrollment action runs as — `{ businessId, accountId, actorUserId, surface }` (`src/app/api/_shared/channels/resolve-actor.ts`), where `surface ∈ {'admin','dashboard'}` reusing `disconnectChannel`'s existing union. It is the **only axis that varies** between the two portals' 12 enrollment routes, which is why it is the seam: `resolveAdminActor` reads `businessId` from the request **body** behind `requireAdminWithAal2`, `resolveOwnerActor` reads `locationId` from the **session** behind `requireRoleOr401` and never from the body — and that asymmetry is a security boundary, not an implementation detail, so the two resolvers must never be collapsed into one function that accepts either source. The resolver also owns each portal's **gate outcome**: admin `start` applies `subscriptionGrantsFullAccess` (via `requireFullAccess`) while admin `cancel`/`disable` deliberately apply none, and owner applies `resolveWritableAccountForLocation` on all three; the runners consume the outcome and never second-guess it (spec §4.2). `surface` is not decoration — it drives the `disconnectChannel` audit tag and the `safeInvalidateChannelCache` label, so `admin.telegram.cancel` and `dashboard.telegram.cancel` stay distinct in the audit log and the cache. _Avoid_: principal, caller identity, auth context, channel session. *(coined in the PR 2 channel-route unification; spec `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-15-portal-audit-remediation-design.md` §4.1)*
CONTEXT.md:37:- **At-risk list**: the `/admin/overview` landing list of businesses that need a look — `OverviewData.atRisk`, built by `getAdminOverview` (`src/lib/admin/overview-service.ts`) and rendered under `At risk (N of M)` beside a `View all →` link to `/admin/businesses`, where N is `atRisk.length` and M is the unfiltered `totalBusinesses`. It is **filtered, then capped, in that order**: eligibility is `churnScore.level !== 'healthy'`, and only then are the top `OVERVIEW_AT_RISK_LIMIT` (**5**) by score kept. It is **not "top 5 by score"** — an all-healthy account yields `[]` and the client renders its `EmptyState`, never five healthy businesses under an "At risk" heading; capping first would surface healthy rows whenever the first five happen to be healthy. Ordering is the pre-existing four-level comparator, moved unchanged: churn score desc → `pendingApprovalCount` desc → name asc → id asc. §5.1's "ties break on name" is the **stability** guarantee (the same data gives the same order across refreshes), not a mandate to drop the pending tier. Ranking is computed in **TypeScript and must never become `ORDER BY … LIMIT`**: `computeChurnRiskScore` is a weighted TS composite and account age contributes, so *every* business carries a non-zero score and no cheap SQL predicate is a provable superset of "at risk" — SQL ranking means reimplementing the risk model in SQL (§5.1). `OVERVIEW_AT_RISK_LIMIT` is declared and exported by the module that applies it, `overview-service.ts`, **not** `src/app/admin/page-sizes.ts` as §5.1 asks: `src/lib/**` may not import `@/app/**` (eslint `APP_LEAF_IMPORT_BAN`), and the disagreement that placement was meant to prevent cannot occur because nothing on the app side reads the number — `page-sizes.ts` carries a pointer comment instead of a second declaration (reasoning: `docs/superpowers/plans/refactor-overview-spec-alignment-deviations.md`). **Overview still reads every business row, and that is a recorded, deliberate limitation** (§5.1), not an oversight: the named rejected alternative is a SQL-ranked attention proxy (past-due, reconnect backlog ≥ 3, oldest pending, stale activity), which is genuinely bounded but drops the churn model from the landing page and would disagree with the Health column on `/admin/businesses`. Trivial at the **Capacity target** (250 locations) — do not "fix" it. _Avoid_: top 5, the churn table (names the deleted paginated `DataTable`), risk list, watchlist. *(coined in PR 3 of the portal audit remediation, 2026-08-15; spec §5.1/§5.3)*
CONTEXT.md:54:- **Ask**: one per-candidate decision inside a batch — **send | withhold**, with a closed withhold-reason enum and the evidence that justifies it. The ask row is the authoritative decision record (it outlives run archival); the per-candidate child run is attribution. In watch-mode "send" means *would send* — nothing is transmitted. "Outreach" is reserved for send-mode's actual sends. *(coined in the #534 executor-shape grilling)*
CONTEXT.md:65:- **Outreach row**: one `solicitation_outreach` row — one dispatch attempt for one **Ask**, carrying the rendered subject/body and the send lifecycle (`delivery_status` over `rendered | queued | sending | sent | withheld | cancelled | failed | unknown`, with a `disposition_reason` on every reason-bearing status and none on `rendered`/`queued`/`sending`/`sent`). This is the send-mode sense watch-mode reserved the word for: the ask says what the restraint policy decided and never changes (090 welded its status to `evidence->>'decision'`), the outreach row says what happened. One live attempt per ask (partial unique over `rendered`/`queued`/`sending`) and attempt numbers are never reused (`UNIQUE (ask_id, attempt_no)`); a regenerate terminalizes the prior row `cancelled`/`superseded` and inserts `attempt_no + 1` in the same transaction. _Avoid_: message row, ask message, send record. *(coined in the #619 executor/approval map; table created by migration 091 in #624)*
CONTEXT.md:66:- **Send batch parent**: the `agent_runs` row of kind `solicitation_send_batch` (`subject_type='solicitation_batch'`) — the sequential sibling of watch-mode's terminal `solicitation_batch` build run. It starts when the send lane engages (generation), spans the approval wait, and terminalizes when the drip closes. Legitimately long-lived but bounded **by construction** at ≤10 days (7-day approval window + 72-hour send window), which is exactly why the spine's `stalled` threshold became kind-aware rather than raised by feel. _Avoid_: send run, drip run. *(coined in #619 D3; ADR 0014)*
CONTEXT.md:67:- **Lazy child**: a per-dispatch-attempt `solicitation_ask` child run created **at release**, not at build — the send-mode counterpart of watch-mode's **Logical fan-out**. Children live minutes (consent recheck → dispatch → provider result → terminal), so no run is ever long-lived enough to be misread as `stalled`, the **Halt latch** has nothing to cancel in the spine, and the join needs no pre-enumeration of children. The plan stays in `solicitation_asks`; the spine records work *performed*, never work *planned* — so the spine deliberately cannot answer "how many children will this batch have." *(coined in #619 D3; ADR 0014)*
CONTEXT.md:68:- **Settled batch**: a batch with no outreach row left in `rendered`, `queued` or `sending` — the join condition, asked of the domain table and never of `agent_runs`. Every writer that terminalizes an **Outreach row** calls `closeBatchIfSettled` in the *same* transaction, so there is no window between terminalizing and joining; it CASes `send_closed_at` on NULL (a second caller writes nothing, not even a duplicate receipt), records one close step, and terminalizes the **Send batch parent** through the disposition lookup (`exhausted`→`completed`; `halted`/`expired`→`cancelled`; `gate_closed`/`generation_failed`→`failed`). The releaser tick is a backstop calling the same function — one implementation, two callers, no join poller. Disposition is an ordered precedence over explicit sources; the status counts are the receipt's *evidence*, not the decision. *(coined in #619 D11/D14; `closeBatchIfSettled` in `src/lib/solicitation/outreach.ts`)*
CONTEXT.md:69:- **Batch state**: the read-time `deriveBatchState(row, now)` over `solicitation_batches`' monotone timestamps — a closed union with fixed precedence `halted > closed > sending > veto_window > approved > expired > built > pending`. There is deliberately **no `status` column**: it would be a second owner of a fact the timestamps already carry, needing its own transition CHECK to stay honest. **This is where watch-mode's deferred `expired` finally lands.** The **Solicitation batch** entry above records that `expired` was specified but never implemented, because watch-mode had nothing for it to gate; here it gates approval — a batch past its 7-day approval window may not be approved by either lane — and such batches are closed through the **Halt latch** with reason `batch_expired`. `built` is the same fact watch-mode's lifecycle names; watch-mode's `reviewed` is not a member of this union, which derives send-lane facts only. *(coined in #619 D2; ADR 0014)*
CONTEXT.md:70:- **Body redaction**: the 90-day post-send NULLing of `rendered_subject`/`rendered_body` **and emptying of `interpolation_inputs` to `'{}'`**, with `body_redacted_at` stamped and the metadata row kept forever — data minimization on third-party PII held on the contact's behalf, timed comfortably past the 14-day attribution window and past bounce/complaint feedback lag. The source goes with the prose: `interpolation_inputs` carries `contact_first_name` and `visit_note`, so redacting only the rendered text would delete the sentence and keep its ingredients. This is enforced in the schema, not merely in the sweep — `solicitation_outreach_body_presence` requires `body_redacted_at IS NULL OR interpolation_inputs = '{}'::jsonb`, so no future writer can stamp a redaction and skip the erasure. It costs the post-90-day reproducibility of the `unverifiable_fact` provenance verdict (#623 §2.4), which was already lost at the same instant anyway: that verdict is computed over `subject + body`, both of which this same sweep deletes. It is **not a delete**, which is why the retention inventory carries it as a novel shape (redact-in-place, plus purge-by-cascade). **Redaction implies TERMINAL, and that is one window on two clocks**: a `sent` row ages from `sent_at`, while a row that settled without ever sending (`withheld`/`cancelled`/`failed`/`unknown`) has no `sent_at` and ages from `rendered_at`. A LIVE row (`rendered`/`queued`/`sending`) is never redactable at any age — a bodyless live attempt can neither send nor settle, and wedges its batch behind `solicitation_outreach_one_live_per_ask`, which is the hazard the rule exists for. #624 shipped the rule as "implies **sent**", which made the stamp unrepresentable on the four never-sent terminals and left their bodies and inputs retained forever (#636 / step-7 S2); 092 widened it to the hazard's actual shape. Swept by two bounded arms of the existing `ArchivalPoller` — no new poller, because the single-replica P2 duplicate-processing budget was already spent — each oldest-first with `SKIP LOCKED`, capped at 5000 rows per cycle and logged as saturated at its own cap. A body is never logged. _Avoid_: body purge, body expiry. *(coined in #619 D6b/D6c; swept in #624; completed in #636)*
CONTEXT.md:71:- **Halt latch**: the single monotone `halted_at`/`halt_reason`/`halted_by` write on `solicitation_batches` that stops a send batch — one mechanism, six reasons (`owner_veto`, `owner_halt`, `admin_halt`, `send_window_elapsed`, `batch_expired`, `platform_halt`). An owner veto inside the window and a mid-drip halt are the *same write with different reasons* — a veto is "halt at zero sends" — and a second halt is a no-op. It cancels outreach rows in `rendered`/`queued` and **never `sending`**: halt means "no further sends," not "undo," and the residual exposure is one child's send-transaction duration, the same honest contract the Reply lane's veto already has. It never terminalizes the parent directly; it calls `closeBatchIfSettled` like any other terminal writer, so parent terminalization keeps one owner. **There is no resume, and it is structurally prevented** — `halted_at` is monotone, the releaser predicate requires `halted_at IS NULL`, and cancelled outreach rows are terminal; to contact those people the owner builds a new batch. **Three writers share ONE private body** — `latchHalt` in `src/lib/solicitation/approval.ts`, which owns the CAS, the outreach cancellations, the audit row, the spine step and the join, in that order: `haltBatch` is the owner arm, its reason derived from **Batch state**; `adminHaltBatch` is the RepWorth operator's, its reason fixed at `admin_halt` by the caller's identity so no request field can name it, requiring a free-text note and writing a `solicitation_batch_admin_halted` row to `account_audit_log` inside the latch's own transaction; `latchSystemHalt` is the third arm, the one with no actor at all, and it carries the three actor-less reasons described below. **Neither human verb latches a `pending` batch**, because latching an unbuilt batch satisfies 091's halt-consistency CHECK through its `built_at IS NULL` arm and then collides with the builder's later `built_at` stamp — the refusal is a lifecycle invariant, not a UI affordance, so it lives on both public verbs rather than on the surface that happens to render a control. Its surface is `POST /api/admin/solicitation/batch/halt`, reached from the batch panel on `/admin/businesses/[id]`. **That panel does not show the newest batch, and the departure is load-bearing rather than cosmetic**: `readAdminBatchSummary` orders on the live-send predicate, then `built_at IS NOT NULL`, then recency. A newer `pending` batch coexists legally with the batch actually dispatching (091's one-active-send index constrains only approved-open-unhalted rows), so recency alone hides the only batch a stop can act on; and because latching is exactly what makes the first key go false, recency alone *then* bounces the panel off the batch just stopped, stranding its success receipt above an unrelated one. Both were real defects, found on staging rather than by review (#865). `send_window_elapsed`, `batch_expired` and `platform_halt` are written by `latchSystemHalt` in `src/lib/solicitation/approval.ts` (#900, third reason #917), the third arm of that one private body: the send window running out, the approval window running out, and a send window running out while sending is halted platform-wide are the same write as an owner veto with nobody to attribute it to, and migration 091's halt-consistency CHECK — widened by 111, which added `platform_halt` to its REASON list and deliberately not to its ACTOR list — makes that structural by forcing `halted_by IS NULL` for exactly those three reasons. **`platform_halt` is not a third window**: it is the SAME `send_window_elapsed` sweep on the SAME 72h clock, reporting a truer cause, because the **Send breaker** and the send window run on different clocks and a trip open for days would otherwise tell every owner approved in the preceding 72 hours that THEIR window elapsed. Which of the two that sweep writes is decided once per tick by `resolveWindowHaltReason` in `src/lib/solicitation/release.ts` — a LABEL-ONLY breaker read, the one sanctioned `try/catch` around `readSendBreaker`, because a rethrow there would stop the sweep and reopen the #915 wedge while the sending gate in phase 4 stays fail-closed and untouched. Its only callers are the release cycle's two sweeps — the send-window sweep (writing `send_window_elapsed` or `platform_halt`) and `batch_expired`, two named arms over ONE shared body, `sweepSystemHalt` (#920), which differ in exactly three values: predicate, isolation label and reason — and they select candidates outside any lock and hold **no** lock across the call — `latchSystemHalt` takes the batch row lock itself (`readBatchForAction`'s `FOR UPDATE OF b`, the ONLY row lock on that path, never a redundant re-take to be deleted) and skips only the ADVISORY lock, which no sweep takes. **It applies no guard list**: the human arms refuse `halted`, `closed` and `pending` because an actor is owed an answer, a clock is owed nothing, and `latchHalt`'s `halted_at IS NULL` CAS is therefore the whole of its idempotence. Sharing the body is also what makes the system arm cancel exactly the rows the owner arm cancels and never a `sending` one. _Avoid_ (**internal vocabulary** — identifiers, comments, docs, run-step and reason values): pause, stop, cancel batch, resume. **Owner-facing copy is carved out for "stop" only** (amended #629): `halt` is domain jargon an owner does not read, and the Requests panel says "Stop this batch" / "Batch stopped" deliberately. The carve-out is exactly one word — `pause`, `resume` and `cancel batch` stay banned everywhere including copy, because each implies a reversibility the latch does not have, and that is what the Avoid list is protecting. Code-level names still obey the term: the panel's mode member is `haltable`, not `stoppable`. *(coined in #619 D10; ADR 0014; copy carve-out #629 D12; admin arm #865)*
CONTEXT.md:72:- **Owner approval**: the `approved_at` / `approval_method='owner'` / `approved_by_user_id` / `veto_deadline_at` write on `solicitation_batches`, CAS'd on `approved_at IS NULL` so a double-submit re-approves nothing and does **not** mint a fresh veto deadline. It may only be taken over a batch whose every send **Ask** already carries a non-cancelled **Outreach row** — the *readiness* rule, re-asserted inside the approval transaction under the batch lock, and the mechanism behind "what was approved is byte-for-byte what sends": no row can render after approval and become releasable unseen. Readiness is **non-cancelled, not `rendered`**, and the difference is the point — a `withheld` row (lint_failed, moderation_high) is a FINISHED attempt with nothing left to render, so it satisfies readiness and is simply not approvable copy; requiring `rendered` would wedge every batch that had one lint failure. Only a `cancelled` row leaves a hole its replacement must fill. **The 15-minute window is an undo, not a review window** — the owner has already read every rendered message, which is exactly why it is short; the auto lane's 60 minutes is anchored differently and belongs to #631. The byte-for-byte guarantee is **unconditional**: amendment B2 once permitted per-ask regeneration after approval, and ADR 0020 (#860) struck that — `regenerateAsk` refuses in every state but `built`, so no path replaces approved bytes. The owner's post-approval recourse is the **Halt latch**, never new bytes — and that recourse costs the whole **cohort**, not just the batch: halting cancels outreach rows but leaves every ask `status='send'`, so `MIN_GAP_DAYS` withholds those contacts as `over_contacted` on any rebuild inside 30 days, by which point `stale_visit` (checked first) has already fired for every vertical but `home_services`. ADR 0020 § "Cost, as verified" carries the file:line derivation; **Ask withdrawal** (#872, ADR 0021) is the cheaper recourse — it costs one contact, not the cohort, and it never adds bytes. _Avoid_: sign-off, confirm batch, release. *(coined in #629; `approveBatch` in `src/lib/solicitation/approval.ts`, executor design D9/D22/D23)*
CONTEXT.md:73:- **Ask exclusion**: the owner deselecting one rendered ask at approval time — `delivery_status='cancelled'`, `disposition_reason='owner_excluded'` (migration **108**, which widened 091's status/reason CHECK; the term existed in the spec but the value did not, so partial approval could not be written at all). Written **in the approval transaction**, never as a separate call, so there is no window in which some rows are cancelled and the batch is not yet approved. It only ever moves toward **restraint** — it removes a message, never adds or alters one — which is why it needs no re-lint and no re-moderation, and why all-or-nothing approval is unnecessary even though D22 forbids editing a rendered ask: the owner's only two verbs are approve and exclude. **Exclusions are validated before any row is written**, and the ordering is load-bearing rather than stylistic: postgres.js commits when the `sql.begin` callback resolves, so an early return after a partial UPDATE would commit that partial cancellation while telling the caller the approval was refused. An unknown or non-live id fails the whole approval closed, touching nothing. Excluding *every* ask is allowed and settles the batch outright — the approval transaction calls `closeBatchIfSettled` like any other terminal writer, so the **Settled batch** join keeps one owner. Its post-approval counterpart is **Ask withdrawal** (#872) — a separate verb with its own reason value, because this term is defined by *when* it is written. _Avoid_: reject ask, skip ask, remove from batch, veto ask. *(coined in #629; executor design D23)*
CONTEXT.md:74:- **Ask regeneration**: the owner asking for a new LLM draft of ONE rendered ask — `regenerateAsk` supersedes the live **Outreach row** (`cancelled`/`superseded`) and inserts its replacement at `attempt_no + 1` in the same transaction, so rejected copy stays auditable and the one-live-attempt index makes the swap atomic. It is **pre-approval only** (ADR 0020, narrowing amendment B2): `built` is the only regenerable **Batch state**, which is what makes **Owner approval**'s byte-for-byte guarantee unconditional. Bounded at **3 per ask per day**, charged per *logical request* (the `job_outbox` row id, so BullMQ replays are free) and enforced in the WORKER — an API-only limiter is bypassable via outbox replay, so the route's check is advisory and fails open while the worker's Lua INCR is authoritative. **A blocked ask is never regenerable** (spec §8.2 r6): regenerating a `withheld` row would launder the fail-closed lint, and clicking three times to escape an `incentive_language` block is the same laundering as an automatic re-attempt, only slower. The replacement inserts **unconditionally of its own verdict**, so a withheld replacement leaves the ask with no live render — terminal for this batch, and the contact stays a candidate for a later one. At most **one regeneration is in flight per batch**, because the enqueue dedups on `(topic, aggregate_id = batchId)`; that is also why "regenerate all" cannot exist as a fan-out. Distinct from **Ask exclusion**, which removes a message, and from owner editing, which D22 forbids outright — the owner may ask for new bytes, never write them. **Where the two terms MEET is load-bearing: a regeneration mints a NEW outreach id, so it invalidates any identifier the panel was holding for that ask.** An exclusion set keyed on `outreachId` therefore silently RE-SELECTED a regenerated contact — the owner's deselection was lost and the message would have sent. Exclusions key on the **ask** id, which survives the swap, and there is deliberately no pruning step; the wire payload resolves ids from the rows on screen, so a dead ask drops out by construction. The same fact bit a second time through a different door (panel state that did not survive the refetch), which is why it is recorded here rather than only in the code (#860 round-1). _Avoid_: edit, rewrite, redraft, retry, re-roll. *(coined in #860; `regenerateAsk` in `src/lib/solicitation/ask-generation.ts`, `requestAskRegeneration` in `src/lib/solicitation/approval.ts`)*
CONTEXT.md:75:- **Ask withdrawal**: the owner cancelling ONE approved Ask's live **Outreach row** after approval — `delivery_status='cancelled'`, `disposition_reason='owner_withdrawn'` (migration **109**, which widened 108's cancel arm by that one value). `owner_excluded` was deliberately NOT reused: **Ask exclusion** is defined by *when* it is written — at approval, in the approval transaction — and a post-approval row that borrowed its value would lie about when and how it was cancelled, the same audit argument that makes the **Halt latch** derive its reason server-side. It is legal in exactly the **Batch states** where the latch would cancel that row — `veto_window`, `approved`, `sending` — and refused everywhere else; the row must be `rendered` or `queued`, **never `sending`** (halt's "no further sends, not undo" contract, inherited whole). It is the Halt latch at ask granularity and Ask exclusion after approval: it only ever moves toward **restraint** — it removes a message, never adds or alters one — so it needs no re-lint, no re-moderation and **no per-ask approval state** (the batch stays approved; `approved_at` and the veto clock are untouched), and it does not reopen ADR 0020, whose whole subject is *replacing* approved bytes: a message that does not send cannot violate **Owner approval**'s byte-for-byte guarantee. **Keyed on the ask, not the row** — the verb resolves the live attempt under the batch lock, so a stale tab still withdraws the right message (the same lesson **Ask regeneration** records for exclusion sets). Same lock order as halt (advisory batch lock → batch row → outreach row → spine), one transaction, one `ask_withdrawn` step on the **Send batch parent**, and `closeBatchIfSettled` like every terminal writer — withdrawing the last live row settles the batch `exhausted`, exactly as excluding every ask at approval does. **It does NOT share the latch's private `latchHalt` body**, and the reason is the term's own boundary rather than a missed refactor: `latchHalt` exists to make the two halt ARMS one write — it CASes `halted_at` on the batch and stamps a halt reason — and withdrawal touches neither, because a batch with one message withdrawn is not a halted batch. Same discipline (lock order, row predicate, settle-through-the-single-owner), different body; the duplicated `delivery_status IN ('rendered','queued')` predicate is a deliberate drift watch, and a THIRD caller is the signal to extract rather than a second (recorded at the #872 branch review). **It does not release the contact**: like a halted ask, the withdrawn ask keeps `status='send'`, so the contact is `over_contacted` for `MIN_GAP_DAYS` — the cost is one contact, not the cohort (ADR 0020 § "Cost, as verified"). Owner-facing word is "Withdraw" (plain English, so no jargon carve-out is needed) behind an inline two-tap confirm — unlike the pre-approval checkbox, this is terminal. _Avoid_: unsend, cancel one, remove, veto ask, un-approve, exclude (post-approval). *(coined in #872; ADR 0021; `withdrawAsk` in `src/lib/solicitation/approval.ts`)*
CONTEXT.md:77:- **Send handoff**: the `enqueueGenerateSolicitationAsks` call at the end of `constructBatch` in `src/lib/solicitation/batch-builder.ts` — the single point where a **Solicitation batch** becomes send-mode work. It is **withheld** — not the batch, and not the individual **Asks** — when the location has no **Attested postal address** or `owner_email_bounced` is TRUE. This is why a missing address is not a **Withhold reason**: it is one per-location fact, not one decision per candidate. The precedence is first-match with `postal_address_missing` ahead of `owner_email_bounced` (it is the CAN-SPAM one), owned by `sendHandoffWithheldReason`. Spec §7.6 point 1 says "no batch produced"; that predates the shared watch/send builder and is **overridden** by §B13 of `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-send-mode-cross-spec-amendments.md` — the batch still builds, the **Spoilage report** is unchanged, and only the handoff is withheld. _Avoid_: send gate, generation gate, dispatch gate (that last is #630's, a different thing). *(coined in #656)*
CONTEXT.md:79:- **Messaging adapter**: the channel-neutral send seam at `src/lib/messaging/messaging-adapter.ts` — **one method, `send`**, taking an `OutboundMessage` and returning the provider message id. `EmailAdapter` (Resend) is its first and today only adapter; **WhatsApp is a later *second adapter*, never a rewrite**, and `getMessagingAdapter()` is where that choice is made so no call site ever names a channel. It mirrors `ReviewPublisher` (`src/lib/publishing/review-publisher.ts`) deliberately — same seam shape, same lazy-singleton selector — because the second-adapter story is exactly the one that seam already proved. **Its first production caller is `dispatchOutreach` (`src/lib/solicitation/dispatch.ts`, #899)** — the seam was hypothetical until then by design, accepted by map decision 14 on one condition: that it stay narrow enough to be cheap to unpick. **#899 exercised it without widening it by one field**, which is the evidence the condition held. Two facts that caller settled and a second tenant will need: the returned `providerMessageId` is persisted in the SAME transaction that CASes the row terminal (so a send can never be recorded without its join key), and the adapter is reached only through `getMessagingAdapter()` inside a bounded in-process retry — every attempt reuses one `OutboundMessage`, so the idempotency key and the body are identical across attempts by construction rather than by discipline. The narrowness is the mitigation: one method, and an `OutboundMessage` whose fields are *derived* from spec §7.1's locked envelope rather than invented, so an adapter that wants a different shape is barely committed to anything. The returned id is not optional decoration — the caller MUST persist it as `solicitation_outreach.provider_message_id`, because it is the only join key the **Solicitation lane** can attribute a bounce or complaint on; a caller that discards it makes every later feedback event unattributable. Solicitation is the seam's first *tenant*, not its owner. _Avoid_: mailer, sender, transport, email service. *(coined in #657)*
CONTEXT.md:80:- **Sending identity**: the solicitation lane's **separate sender** — three things that only mean anything together: the `reviews@feedback.<env>.repworth.net` mailbox, the `feedback.` sending domain (its own Resend domain, its own DKIM `d=`, its own DMARC record), and the domain-scoped `SOLICITATION_RESEND_API_KEY`. **Deliberately NOT the transactional identity**, and the reason is a security boundary rather than tidiness: a key leaked from this lane *cannot forge an approval email*, which would otherwise be a path around invariants #3 and #5 reached sideways, through the mail provider instead of through the approval code. The separation is only as real as its fail-closed contract, so **every solicitation config var fails closed** — unset means **no sends**, never a degraded send on the transactional key (`getSolicitationResend()` throws; `resolveFromAddress()` throws; the `'solicitation'` header category throws). The mailbox is **monitored, never `no-reply@`** — setting it creates a servicing obligation (reply-based opt-outs enter the **Suppression ledger** manually inside CAN-SPAM's 10 business days), and it is a *different* mailbox from the `SOLICITATION_UNSUBSCRIBE_MAILTO` address advertised in `List-Unsubscribe`, which carries its own daily ritual. Isolation is strong but **not total**: `feedback.repworth.net` is a child of the transactional apex, not a sibling, so organizational-domain reputation still carries some weight (spec FU-1). Stood up by `docs/runbooks/solicitation-sending-domain.md` — authored at #657, **executed at #633**. _Avoid_: from address, sender config, mail identity. *(coined in #657)*
CONTEXT.md:81:- **Solicitation lane**: the branch of the Resend webhook ingress selected by `data.tags.lane === 'solicitation'` — **the discriminant is read before any DB lookup**, chosen over a try-table-A-fall-back-to-B chain because that chain couples the two lanes and makes a genuinely-unknown `email_id` indistinguishable from a lane mismatch. Its sibling is the untouched `notification_deliveries` path; one endpoint, one svix secret, two lanes. **Per rule DR1 the lane is not flag-gated and must never become so** — `SOLICITATION_SEND_ENABLED` masters *sending* only, so the lane, the unsubscribe ingress and the `/u/<token>` landing page all stay fully live when the flag is off; a webhook arriving for mail already sent has to be recorded regardless, and adding a gate would silently drop opt-out-bearing events. Its **write surface is deliberately narrow**: only the async signal columns migration 091 calls "columns, NOT statuses" — `bounced_at`, `complained_at`, **`bounce_kind`** (#898), `provider_error` — plus the **Suppression ledger**. `bounce_kind` (`hard` | `soft`, migration 110) is a widening of exactly one column and of that same async-signal class, not a new kind of write: it records the bounce classification Resend already sends, so the **Send breaker** can count hard bounces as a fact rather than a guess. **`provider_error` could not serve** — `recordFailure` COALESCEs `failed:<reason>` into that same column, so an `email.failed` landing before `email.bounced` would mask a real hard bounce, which is the fail-*open* direction on a governor specified to fail closed. It never writes `delivery_status` or `sent_at` (#630 owns the send lifecycle, and a webhook write would race 091's ladder CHECKs), and never `unsubscribed_at` (the RFC 8058 one-click POST lands on `worker/ingress/unsubscribe.ts`). Unmatched attribution splits by event rather than uniformly: suppression-bearing events (permanent bounce, complaint, `email.suppressed`) **write the recipient's global opt-out from the SIGNED payload and ack**, while soft bounces and `email.failed` warn and ack. The suppression-bearing arm used to **throw → 503 → Resend retries**; #967 retired that because a retry can only ever close one of the three unmatched causes. The other two are permanent: an **account purge** deletes the `solicitation_contacts` row `loadAttribution` joins, and a send whose terminal CAS lost never persisted a `provider_message_id` at all — so the retry looped until the 300s signature window 401'd it, and the recipient's global opt-out, the one fact the purge's suppression contract promises to preserve, was never written. The fallback normalizes the echoed recipient through the one owner (`normalizeEmail`) and writes `accountId: null`, which is that contract's boundary rule stated exactly. **Exactly one address in `to`, or throw**: a global row blocks that person for every tenant, so the lane refuses to guess rather than suppressing whatever the payload held. Two costs, both accepted at #967: in the seconds-wide race before #630 commits `provider_message_id` the ledger row is still right but the outreach `bounced_at`/`complained_at` stamp is lost, so the **Send breaker** undercounts one bounce; and the recipient address the ack leaves in `inbound_events.payload` is bounded only by `ArchivalPoller`'s 30-day sweep of `processed` rows — which is itself a gain, since the old `failed` rows were never swept at all. **Acking also removed these events from `checkFailedInboundEvents`' `status='failed'` alert set, so the fallback raises its own Sentry warning** (`reportUnattributedSuppression`): a log line nothing subscribes to does not replace a pager, and a sustained rate here is the detector for #965's still-unexplained loss of Outreach rows. **Never normalize the echo while attribution exists**: the stored contact handle wins outright, and re-deriving one beside it mints a second key for the same person. _Avoid_: solicitation webhook, ask webhook, second handler. *(coined in #657; unmatched-attribution rule re-anchored at #967)*
CONTEXT.md:83:- **Send breaker trip**: one `solicitation_send_breaker` row — `tripped_at`, `trip_reason` (`hard_bounce_rate` | `complaint_rate`, in that fixed precedence so the page names the metric to fix first), and the `metrics` **Breaker window** snapshot that tripped it, frozen at the instant of the trip and never recomputed. **At most one trip is open at a time**, and that invariant lives in a partial unique index rather than in application code, so a second breached poller tick is an `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` no-op — one trip, one row, however long the breach stands. **Paging is LEVEL-triggered and the write being a no-op does not make it edge-triggered**: the poller probes for an open trip before it measures anything and re-pages on every tick while one stands, because an open breaker is a STATE and one dropped event must never equal a silent platform-wide halt. The repeats carry a constant per-reason title under the same routing key, so Sentry collapses them into one issue that keeps receiving events. Resetting stamps `reset_at`/`reset_by`/`reset_note` on the same row — the table is append-only, so trips are a history, not a current-state flag. A **mandatory reset note** is enforced by 110's `reset_consistency` CHECK as well as by the route schema, so "who cleared this, and why" cannot be unanswerable by omission. _Avoid_: breaker event, breaker state row, incident. *(coined in #898)*
CONTEXT.md:85:- **Dispatch gate**: the union of per-child checks #630 runs **inside one child's short pre-send transaction**, immediately before the physical send — the last thing that can stop one message. Stated once in **B6** of `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-28-send-mode-cross-spec-amendments.md` (itself the union of #619 D18 as amended by #620 §6.3, #623's two re-checks and #622's byte-match). B6 numbers nine items, but **two of them are not per-child work and the gate does not run them**: item 7 (daily caps) is checked at release and the gate only asserts the release stamped `queued_at`, and amendment **B14** moved item 3 (`subscriptionGrantsFullAccess`) to release level too. The seven the child actually runs, in order: batch not halted and send window open → `SOLICITATION_SEND_ENABLED` → **suppression** lookup → consent row + `SEND_ELIGIBLE_ATTESTATION_VERSIONS` → postal address exists and byte-matches the stored footer → re-lint at current `ASK_LINT_VERSION` → re-moderation. **It is per-CHILD and therefore per-CONTACT**, which is the whole boundary: an account-level fact has no business here because every child of a batch computes the same answer for it, so account-level facts are **release-level gates** — the fixed order **Send breaker → platform ceiling → account cap → Dispatch gate** (#622 §4.3) runs the first three once per release, before any child exists. B14's move is that boundary applied: as a per-child item, a lapsed subscription had to make the child *defer*, but #900 CASes the **Outreach row** `rendered → queued` **before** the child runs, so a deferring child stranded a `queued` row the release predicate could never select again. Its dispatch-time vocabulary is a **row** fact, never a location fact: `no_postal_address` and `postal_address_mismatch` (migration 110's two new `withheld` members) say what was true of THIS attempt, and are a different fact from the **Send handoff**'s `postal_address_missing`, which is one handoff-time fact about the business. **Its writer is `applyDispatchGate` / `dispatchOutreach` in `src/lib/solicitation/dispatch.ts` (#899)**, which ships without a migration because 110 already held the vocabulary. Three implementation facts B6 does not carry, each load-bearing: (a) the two SLOW items are gathered **above** the transaction with no lock held — re-moderation is a ~10s provider call and the repo rule forbids holding a lock across one; it is safe only because ADR 0020 makes stored bytes immutable after approval, so the verdict cannot go stale, and **B6's ORDER is unchanged — only the gathering moved**. (b) The gate is **never re-run on a row already `sending`**: recording `withheld` for a message that may already have reached a real person is the same lie-with-teeth D12d cites for `unknown`. (c) Its two **defer** items defer for different reasons — `batch_open` because the **Halt latch** sweep already cancelled the row, `send_flag` because 091/110 carry no `flag_off` member and inventing one would terminalize a row the owner never withdrew. _Avoid_: send gate, pre-send check, dispatch check, per-child gate (that last names the position, not the term), release gate (those are the three governors ABOVE it). *(named in #619 D18; stated once in amendment B6; boundary, item count and provenance recorded in #898; writer + implementation facts in #899)*
CONTEXT.md:88:- **Veto anchor**: the release-cycle phase — wired immediately BEFORE the release phase in `release.ts`'s `runReleaseCycle` — that CASes `veto_deadline_at` **ONCE** from `MIN(notification_deliveries.sent_at)` (computed IN SQL, never a JS `Date` round-trip — the #520 microsecond-truncation gotcha) plus the granted `veto_window_minutes` recorded on the **Send batch parent**'s LATEST `batch_approved` spine step, and writes a `veto_window_granted` step (first send time, deadline, actor `system`). **Fail-closed per D9: no sent Veto notice, no anchor, no release, ever** — an `auto_policy` batch with zero delivered notices simply never gets a deadline, and the batch eventually dies through the `send_window_elapsed` sweep's expiry closure, not through this phase. It is a **phase, not a poller** — `anchorVetoDeadlines` (`src/lib/solicitation/auto-approval.ts`) is one more caller of the same `tallyPhase`/`alertPhaseFailure` seam every other `release.ts` phase uses, isolating one failing candidate to itself (a Sentry `warning` plus a log line) rather than the whole tick. _Avoid_: deadline sweep, anchor poller (it is a phase, not a poller). *(coined in #631 decision 4; `anchorVetoDeadlines` in `src/lib/solicitation/auto-approval.ts`)*
CONTEXT.md:90:- **Attributed review**: a review recorded against **exactly one** **Outreach row** by the name matcher — migration 114's `attributed_review_id` / `attributed_at` / `attribution_rule` / `attribution_detail`, plus one `review_attributed` outcome on that row's **Lazy child**. It is a **shadow** metric and is **never billed** until May §6.4's M3 precision bar clears (map #617 decision 1), because D16d is binding: a Google review carries a display name and a date and there is no deterministic contact-to-review identity seam, so this is evidence about the matcher, not a settled fact about the customer. The "has this row ever been attributed" marker is **`attributed_at`, never `attributed_review_id`** — the FK is `ON DELETE SET NULL`, so deleting a review clears the id and leaves the timestamp, and all four sites (candidate query, enqueue guard, index predicate, final CAS) key on the timestamp. `attribution_detail` carries NO personal data, for the same retention reason **Body redaction** exists. _Avoid_: won review, earned review, converted review, conversion. *(coined in #632)*
CONTEXT.md:91:- **Attribution match**: the two-rule name test — `full_name` and `first_last_initial` — that IS the matcher; there is no score, no threshold and no fallback (deviation D1, which is why the column stores the rule NAME). Both sides need **at least two tokens**, first and last must each be *compatible*, and at least **one** position must be a FULL-token match. **Compatible means equal OR one side is an initial the other starts with, on either position** — so `m. okafor` matches `michael okafor` and is recorded as `first_last_initial`; the narrower reading in which only exact first tokens match was considered and **rejected** (owner, 2026-08-24), and the `isInitial` arm of `compatible` must keep both directions. The full-token requirement is what stops `d. rodriguez` matching `dana r.` on two initials and no evidence; middles are ignored, so `dana m rodriguez` still matches `dana rodriguez`. _Avoid_: fuzzy match, name score, similarity, confidence. *(coined in #632)*
CONTEXT.md:92:- **Ambiguous attribution**: two or more **Outreach rows** matching one review. It attributes **nothing** — a shadow metric that guesses is worse than one that abstains. **The abstention is LOGGED, not recorded** (branch-review M4, and the distinction is load-bearing): `attributeReview` returns early on every non-`attributed` resolution and writes no row, so `ambiguous`, `no_match` and `no_candidates` exist only as `attribution_resolved` lines in the worker's stdout — bounded retention, nothing SQL can join against `solicitation_outreach`. Only `attributed` has a durable row. Its RATE is the stated input to May §6.4's M3 precision bar, so **make the abstention durable before relying on that rate, or run the analysis inside the log-retention window**; a follow-up PR owns the durable event, which needs `src/lib/analytics/events.ts` and could not land inside #632's edit surface. The log line carries `resolution` and the candidate count, never the reviewer name. It is not an error and never fails the job: `no_candidates` and `no_match` are equally normal. _Avoid_: unresolved match, tie, conflict, failed attribution. *(coined in #632)*
CONTEXT.md:94:## Restraint policy — locked 2026-07-23, ticket #533
CONTEXT.md:96:- **Restraint policy**: the deterministic, sentiment-blind rule set that decides send vs withhold for each Solicitation candidate — the refusal logic is the product; everything else displays its decisions. No LLM in the decision path. *(coined in the §9 solicitation roadmap; locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:97:- **Withhold reason**: one code from the closed six-reason enum (`no_consent`, `suppressed`, `already_reviewed`, `stale_visit`, `over_contacted`, `velocity_banked`) explaining why a candidate was not asked. Every reason is sentiment-blind by construction; `recent_negative` is permanently rejected (ADR 0012). First-match precedence; one reason per withhold. _Avoid_: refusal code, skip reason. *(locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:101:- **Evidence snapshot**: the immutable, per-withhold record (typed per reason, `policy_version` + `decided_at` envelope, candidate referenced by ID only, never duplicated PII, never a star rating) that makes every decision re-explainable without re-running the policy. *(locked in ticket #533)*
CONTEXT.md:102:- **Spoilage report**: the batch-approval display of withheld candidates with their withhold reasons — the visible-restraint aha moment of watch-mode. *(coined in the §9 solicitation roadmap)*
CONTEXT.md:107:- **Named rival**: the single local-pack competitor the Competitor Delta line reports on — the owner's pick where one exists, otherwise the Auto-default rival. A pick survives the rival's absence from the pack: an absent pick reports the dropout ("dropped out of the local pack") while the absence spans the latest snapshot window — so the note repeats across weekly digests that read the same biweekly window — then the line falls back to the Auto-default rival until the pick reappears; a pick is never auto-removed. Mechanics: the pick is stored per location as `businesses.rival_place_id` + `rival_title_snapshot`; the live pack title wins whenever the pick is present (a renamed rival reports its current name), and the stored snapshot is only used to name the dropout. Dropout is derived, never stored — a pick absent from the latest pack but present in the immediately preceding one, read from the same two-snapshot window that drives velocity; a pick present with a NULL review count is not a dropout (it fell back, nothing left), and a pack recorded empty for the latest date never raises one. *(coined in #532; pick mechanics locked in #546)*
CONTEXT.md:109:- **Competitor pack**: the full ~20-entry `local_results` set persisted as `competitor_snapshots` rows per (business, keyword, search_location, snapshot_date) — is_self row included when the location appears, entries with missing place_id/rating/review_count kept with NULLs. Replaced atomically on re-run (replace-pack idempotency); an empty pack for a date is itself a recorded fact. *(coined in #538 grilling)*
CONTEXT.md:116:- **Consent attestation**: the per-upload record — attesting user id, timestamp, versioned attestation-text constant — required as a blocking step at CSV upload; contact/visit rows inherit its provenance via their upload FK. It is NOT a per-row consent claim — per-handle consent state is the **Consent ledger**'s job (below, #625). *(coined in ticket #535 grilling; per-row semantics resolved by #625)*
CONTEXT.md:122:records what the owner asserted, this records what the recipient revoked — and the second
CONTEXT.md:125:- **Suppression ledger**: the `solicitation_suppressions` table (migration 096, #643) — one row per opt-out, keyed on the **Normalized handle** per channel and never on the contact row id, so delete-and-re-upload cannot launder it (#535 §6). Rows are **first-write-wins** (`ON CONFLICT … DO NOTHING` against two partial unique indexes, each predicate re-spelled byte-for-byte): a `complaint` arriving after an `unsubscribe_link` leaves the earlier row alone, because the state is already correct and the history belongs in the provider webhook records #622 §5 already stores. Seven closed sources (§3.1) — `unsubscribe_one_click`, `unsubscribe_link`, `bounce_permanent`, `complaint`, `provider_suppressed`, `admin_manual`, `owner_manual` — exported as `as const` arrays by the single authoritative `src/lib/solicitation/suppression.ts`, never string literals at call sites. Soft bounces, `delivery_delayed`, `email.failed` and any ambiguous bounce classification **never** suppress (§3.2); `provider_suppressed` is the known-dirty source, because Resend's list is team-wide across every domain and API key including transactional (§3.3). **No expiry, ever** — a TTL on an opt-out is a violation with a cron job attached. Both evaluation points are **existence checks**, never a comparison against `suppressed_at`, which deliberately sidesteps the postgres.js microsecond-truncation class. The handle is stored **raw, not hashed** (§6.4). RLS deny-all — server-owned in the `auto_approval_policies` posture (invariant #14), because a direct client read would expose the `account_id` provenance §7.4 forbids surfacing. Two derived rules bound it: **DR1** — `SOLICITATION_SEND_ENABLED` masters *sending* only, so flipping it off leaves every ledger write, the ingress and the landing page fully live. **DR2** — the ledger is **solicitation-only**; a transactional or notification path that consults it stops delivering approval emails to an owner who opted out as somebody else's customer, reaching invariant #3 sideways. _Avoid_: blocklist, do-not-mail flag, opt-out table, suppression list. *(coined in #621 grilling; schema + domain core by #643, PR #647)*
CONTEXT.md:126:- **Suppression scope**: the `scope ∈ {'global','account'}` discriminator recording **who authored the opt-out**, not how widely it happens to apply. `global` is the recipient's — or the mail ecosystem's on their behalf — keyed `(channel, normalized_handle)` across every tenant, legitimate because the recipient spoke and defensible under one shared sender (`reviews@feedback.repworth.net`), where a complaint scores against the domain no matter which business sent. `account` is the owner's `owner_manual` do-not-contact, keyed `(channel, normalized_handle, account_id)`; global would let Owner A silently degrade Owner B's reach with no recipient having said anything — a cross-tenant write dressed as a product feature. The source/scope pairing is **enforced by a DB CHECK** (G11), because `owner_manual + global` would otherwise insert cleanly; it is **one-directional** — `scope='account'` requires `account_id NOT NULL`, while a `global` row MAY still carry `account_id` as provenance. **Lookup contract**: a handle is suppressed for an account iff a `global` row exists for `(channel, handle)` OR an `account` row exists for `(channel, handle, account_id)` — owned by the one exported fragment `suppressionMatchSql` and never re-implemented at a call site (G7: no `isSuppressed()` helper, because the second consumer does not exist yet). **Global wins** when both match (G3): reporting the account row would let an owner clear it under §7.6, watch the contact stay suppressed, and have no idea why. Retention splits on the same axis (D7) — global rows are `allowed_limited_retention` and survive an account purge with `account_id` intact, account rows hard-delete. _Avoid_: per-account suppression, tenant scope, suppression level. *(coined in #621 grilling; D1/D5)*
CONTEXT.md:128:- **Unsubscribe redemption**: exchanging an Unsubscribe token for a `scope='global'` Suppression ledger row — the only thing #644 does. **Claim first, then suppress**: a single `UPDATE … SET redeemed_at = NOW() WHERE token_hash = $1 AND redeemed_at IS NULL RETURNING account_id, normalized_handle, channel` is the one write-ordering primitive, and `recordSuppression` is fed from the provenance that statement locked, never from a value read earlier. **`business_id` is deliberately NOT returned** — `recordSuppression` has no field for it, so claiming it would be provenance the ledger cannot record, and G12's one-way implication (`account_id IS NULL` ⇒ `business_id IS NULL`) already makes it redundant for the null case. Ordering it the other way round lets a replayed spent link silently undo an audited **Correction path** removal, and lets a concurrent purge write a suppression carrying stale non-null provenance against §5.2. Four closed outcomes, and the third is the one that is easy to lose: `redeemed` · `already_suppressed` · `already_redeemed_not_suppressed` · `unknown_token`. **A spent token is not proof of a live suppression** — `redeemed_at IS NOT NULL` proves only that it was *once* redeemed, so the spent branch reads the ledger explicitly; with no live global row the outcome is `already_redeemed_not_suppressed`, which mints no receipt and deliberately does **not** re-suppress. This is checked against a global-only existence query rather than `suppressionMatchSql`, which answers a different question (may we contact this handle *for this account*) and would be satisfied by an `account`-scoped row that makes the success sentence false. **Honest failure**: a token that does not resolve returns `unknown_token` rather than a success-shaped no-op, because reporting success on a #630 mint race is precisely the lost opt-out this ticket exists to prevent. _Avoid_: consume the token, burn the link, mark unsubscribed. *(coined in #621 grilling; ordering locked across review rounds 2–4 of #644)*
CONTEXT.md:131:- **Correction path**: the **admin-only, audited removal** of a suppression row that is factually wrong — an admin typo on a manual entry, an ambiguous bounce the #622 §5.3 fail-safe did not catch, or a `provider_suppressed` mirror that originated on the transactional side (§3.3). Framed as **error correction, never re-subscribe**: not exposed to owners, not to recipients, no self-serve entry point, and it writes an audit record naming who removed the row and why. It exists because "no re-subscribe" without it is itself a defect — a typo would be permanently unfixable and the answer to "please put me back" would be "we cannot, ever," a worse story than the one the rule was avoiding. **The sink is a `solicitation_suppression_corrections` row (migration 098), not `account_audit_log`** (P-1): it takes `writeAccountAuditLog`'s *posture* — the record lands with the act it describes — but deliberately not its table, because `account_audit_log.account_id` is `NOT NULL REFERENCES accounts(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` and the rows §8.2 exists to correct carry **no account** (an `admin_manual` global row entered from a forwarded mailbox reply names none; neither does a `provider_suppressed` mirror originating on the transactional side), while a CASCADE would additionally delete the compliance record along with the account it merely names as provenance. Atomicity is **structural, not conventional**: `correctSuppression` is ONE data-modifying CTE whose `DELETE … RETURNING` feeds the INSERT (P-9), so the delete cannot commit without its record even if a call site forgets a transaction, and a row already gone returns `not_found` rather than an orphan record. The table carries **no FKs at all** (P-2) — a compliance record must outlive every referent it names, and the row it describes is deleted by the same statement that writes it — but it **does** snapshot the corrected row's `normalized_handle`, `account_id`, `created_by_user_id` and `note` (P-3, D-A11). Those four are the evidence behind the assertion being corrected: `suppression_id` points at a deleted row, so without the handle the record cannot identify its subject, and without the creator and the §7.3 originating message reference it cannot show the original entry was legitimate. `account_id` is not merely provenance — for a `scope='account'` row it **is** the Suppression scope key (§1.2, non-null by construction), so omitting it left account-scoped corrections unable to say whose do-not-contact was removed. Carrying that evidence makes the table account-linked, so it is **classified** — `retain_compliance_limited_pii`, the disposition its siblings `solicitation_suppressions` and `solicitation_unsubscribe_tokens` already carry (D7) — rather than renamed to evade classification: `original_*`-style column names slip past `accountLinkedTablesFromMigrations()` on a word-boundary technicality, and naming data to dodge a governance check is worse than the gap it hides. Distinct from **§7.6 owner clearing**, where an owner removes an `account`-scoped row they created themselves because no recipient ever spoke; that act writes to **`account_audit_log`** instead (P-4), since it is account-scoped with a non-null `account_id` and is emphatically not the compliance act — different act, different sink, deliberately not interchangeable. An owner may **never** clear a `global` row, and the UI must not present the two controls as the same thing. _Avoid_: unsuppress, re-subscribe, opt back in, restore contact. *(coined in #621 grilling; D6 §8.2; shipped by #645 — migration 098, `src/lib/admin/suppressions-service.ts`, the AAL2-gated `/admin/suppressions` console under **Safety**. The P-n citations above are #645's plan decision register, `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-03-645-manual-suppression-paths.md`)*
CONTEXT.md:132:- **Attestation never overrides suppression**: stated verbatim because it is the most likely way self-serve re-subscribe gets built by accident — an owner re-uploads a CSV, re-attests consent, and someone reasons "they attested, so we're covered." **Attestation is the owner asserting a relationship; suppression is the recipient revoking one. The second outranks the first, permanently.** Consent and suppression are independent inputs to `evaluateAsk`; the withhold-reason precedence (`no_consent` before `suppressed`) governs only **which reason is reported when both apply** and is not an override relationship — it will be misread as one unless this is written down. The mechanism backing the rule is #535 §6's handle keying, which is why an opt-out cannot be laundered by delete-and-re-upload. Its build-time expression is **populate, do not pre-filter** (§4.2): the batch build stamps `candidate.suppression` and `evaluateAsk` owns the withhold, so the shipped precedence-2 `suppressed` gate never becomes dead code. _Avoid_: consent overrides, re-consent, re-permission. *(coined in #621 grilling; D6 §8.1)*
CONTEXT.md:137:- **QR redirect**: the public hop `GET /q/<businessId>` that resolves a scan server-side and 302s to `https://search.google.com/local/writereview?placeid=<validated place id>`. Resolution lives in `src/lib/locations/review-qr.ts` (`resolveQrRedirect` — pure result object, no side effects); the route orchestrates only. The gate is the named predicate `subscriptionAllowsQrRedirect` = `{active, trialing, past_due}` (invariant #11 — `past_due` stays alive so a card failure never kills a printed counter code mid-grace); `pending_payment`/`cancelled`, archived, missing rows, and non-canonical place ids all render the scanner-facing unavailable page (scanner copy, no dashboard CTA) with one reason-coded log line — never a redirect, never an error leak. Each successful redirect best-effort-records one `qr_scan_redirected` analytics event AFTER resolve; an insert failure never blocks the 302. `Cache-Control: no-store` on every response. _Avoid_: QR landing page (there is none — the hop is a straight 302), scan tracker, short link. *(coined in feat-qr-review-request grilling; gate set chosen by owner 2026-08-26)*
CONTEXT.md:150:- **Admin invite**: an **Account** minted by staff at `/admin/businesses/new` from exactly three facts — owner email, owner name, business name — whose owner then walks the *same* self-serve funnel every other owner walks. The durable marker is `businesses.onboarded_by IS NOT NULL`, and it is deliberately the **only** marker: no per-account comp flag, no invite table, no parallel journey. That single column carries three separate meanings downstream (who provisioned the row, the **Beta comp coupon**'s eligibility test, and the `CleanupAbandonedSignupsPoller`'s exclusion), which is why widening or reusing it is a bigger act than it looks. The form collects nothing else because everything it used to collect now has a better owner: the location comes from the owner at `/onboarding/start`, voice from the wizard, tier from `/onboarding/pay` (created on the `copilot` default). The pre-2026-08-14 shape — a 4-section form ending in a dead-end handoff screen, activated by a human out of band — is **gone**, including `pending_payment_admin_handoff` and `PendingAdminHandoffScreen`; a doc that still names them is stale. _Avoid_: hand-onboarding, manual activation, admin-activated account, "the handoff" (all name the deleted flow). *(coined in the admin-invite-funnel plan, steps 3–4 and 8)*
CONTEXT.md:151:- **Checkout attempt**: the **Account**-owned durable lease in `accounts.checkout_attempt_*` (id, tier, variant, price ids, expiry — and since migration **105**, `checkout_attempt_coupon_id`). Every Stripe Session parameter is built from that **immutable snapshot** under a per-attempt idempotency key, never from a live env or config read. The snapshot is the point: a Session created under key *K* must be reproducible from key *K* forever, so any input that could change between the attempt's creation and its retry has to be frozen INTO the attempt or it becomes a way to send Stripe different params under the same key. **That is why the coupon had to join it** — `BETA_COMP_COUPON_ID` is an env var, and an ops flip between an owner's first click and their retry would otherwise silently change the price under a key Stripe treats as identical. It also fixes the decision's *locus*: the coupon is resolved once inside `applyTierSnapshot`'s transaction while the activation lock is held, so eligibility is read at one instant against one primary Business, rather than re-derived at each session creation from whatever the environment says now. An attempt is complete-or-cleared as a **whole** (`accounts_checkout_attempt_complete_check`, 077 as rewritten by 105): the cleared branch enumerates every attempt column, so a cleared attempt cannot retain a coupon snapshot; the active branch leaves the coupon nullable, because NULL there is not a missing value but the explicit *no-coupon* decision recorded for every non-invitee. _Avoid_: checkout session (that is Stripe's object, downstream of this), checkout lease, pending checkout, "the tier snapshot" (that names one field of it). *(coined in the admin-invite-funnel plan step 2; columns from migration 077, coupon from 105)*
CONTEXT.md:155:## Refusal Log — locked 2026-08-29 (plan `docs/superpowers/plans/feat-refusal-log-plan.md`)
CONTEXT.md:157:- **Refusal**: something RepWorth **decided not to do** and already recorded — a draft held for the owner, an auto-post revoked before it posted, an autonomy suspension, an Ask withheld at build, an Outreach withheld at send, a batch we halted. It is a *decision*, which is what excludes every neighbour that looks like one: an **owner act** (`owner_veto`, `owner_halt`, `owner_excluded`, `owner_withdrawn`) is the owner's decision and reporting it back as our restraint lies about who decided; a **delivery failure** (`send_failed`, `send_exhausted`) is an act that did not land; an **elapsed clock** (`batch_expired`, `send_window_elapsed`) weighed nothing; `superseded` is a replacement, `send_indeterminate` is an unknown, and `velocity_banked` is volume shaping the next batch is expected to return. **The one refusal we cannot explain is also the highest-volume one.** `AutoApprovalBlockReason` — 22 members at `worker/jobs/response-generation/auto-approval.ts:37` — is the per-review answer to "we drafted a reply and did not auto-post it, why", and it reaches only a `logger.log` call at `persist.ts:570-576`; the `policy_gate` run-spine step records `decision: 'held_for_owner'` with no reason. **So the Refusal Log reports THAT a draft was held for the owner, never WHY.** Closing that needs a capture change and is deliberately out of scope — say so, never paper over it. **A judge verdict is NOT a refusal**: the Judge runs on already-posted replies (`judge-store.ts:115`, `WHERE r.status = 'posted'`), so a verdict is a post-publication observation; counting one would be false AND would double-count the autonomy suspension a flag triggers. _Avoid_: skip, block, rejection, failure, "blocked reason" (the last names the unavailable `AutoApprovalBlockReason`, not this). *(coined in the refusal-log grilling; scope corrections D3a–D3d)*
CONTEXT.md:158:- **Refusal category**: one of the closed seven, in precedence order — `unsafe_content`, `watchdog_unavailable`, `below_our_bar`, `no_permission`, `wrong_moment`, `autonomy_paused`, `missing_information` — owned by `src/lib/refusals/vocabulary.ts` on the **Withhold reason** model (`WITHHOLD_REASONS`: closed `as const` array, union derived from it, owner labels keyed `Record<ClosedEnum, Meta>` so a drifted enum is a compile error). Every source code maps to exactly one category **or to explicit `null`**, and `null` is the recorded decision "not a refusal", **never an omission** — a member with no entry is a member nobody decided about, which is why `Object.hasOwn` and not truthiness is the test. Four vocabularies are guarded by `satisfies Record<SourceEnum, RefusalCategory | null>` (`WithholdReason`, `AutoPolicyDowngradeReason`, `AutonomySuspensionTrigger`, `RenderWithholdReason`); the batch halt list and the full outreach disposition vocabulary have **no TypeScript owner** — their authority is the migration 111 CHECK — so they are declared as closed constants and guarded by a test that parses `supabase/migrations/111_platform_halt_reason.sql`. **An unrecognised code is a fault, not a category**: `refusalCategoryFor` throws `UnknownRefusalCodeError` rather than defaulting, because a default files an unmapped refusal under a plausible-looking heading and the owner cannot tell the difference. _Avoid_: refusal type, refusal code (that is the **Refusal source**'s verbatim value), bucket, reason group. *(locked in the refusal-log plan §3)*
CONTEXT.md:159:- **Refusal source**: the second axis — the **lane** a refusal came from plus the **verbatim code** it carries inside that lane, so one code can mean different things in different lanes and the map is per-lane rather than global. Six lanes: `held_draft` (S1 `flagged_responses`), `auto_post_revoked` (S3 `approval_audit_log`), `autonomy_suspended` (S4 `account_audit_log`, `event_type='autonomy_suspended'`), `ask_withheld` (S5 `solicitation_asks`), `outreach_withheld` (S6 `solicitation_outreach`, **withheld arm only**), `batch_halted` (S7 `solicitation_batches`). There is no S2 — rev 1's was `judge_verdicts`, dropped. Two traps the per-lane split exists for: **`held_draft` has no code axis at all** (`flagged_responses.reason` is moderation prose that may never leave the server, and the row exists only for a `high`-severity flag, so the lane itself is the whole fact); and **`admin_halt`/`platform_halt` mean opposite things in two lanes** — `latchHalt` writes a batch's halt reason through to every cancelled Outreach row, so those copies map to `null` while the same codes on `batch_halted` map to `autonomy_paused`. Counting both would report one halt once at the batch and again per recipient. **S4 is account-wide**: `account_audit_log` has no location key, a suspension applies to every location and therefore appears in every location scope — joining it through `businesses` multiplies one audit row by the location count. _Avoid_: refusal origin, source table (the lane is named for the act, not the table), event type. *(locked in the refusal-log plan §2–§3; S4 re-sourced by D3b, S7 added by D3d)*
CONTEXT.md:160:- **Restraint tab**: the second of the **two** tabs on `/dashboard/analytics` — `Metrics` and `Restraint` — routed at `/dashboard/analytics/restraint`, stating the window's total refusal count and its breakdown by **Refusal category**, newest first within each. **Links only, no writes** — a held draft links to its review (and shows no draft text, no review text and no moderation reason), `autonomy_paused` links to `/settings/auto-approval`, `no_permission` links to the contacts list, and `wrong_moment`/`below_our_bar`/`missing_information`/`watchdog_unavailable` carry none. Fed by ONE `RefusalLog` object so the stated total and the category counts cannot disagree. The empty window reads as a plain sentence, never an error or an empty grid. It is **display only**: this feature records no new refusal, changes no gate, and adds no migration. _Avoid_: refusals dashboard, restraint report (that is the **Spoilage report**, a different surface on `/dashboard/requests`), "what we blocked". *(locked in the refusal-log intake `user_visible_behavior`)*
