+> `WHERE r.status = 'posted'`), so counting one would be false AND would
+> double-count the autonomy suspension a flag triggers. And **the highest-volume
+> refusal is unavailable**: `AutoApprovalBlockReason` (22 members,
+> `worker/jobs/response-generation/auto-approval.ts:37`) reaches only a
+> `logger.log` at `persist.ts:570-576`, so the log reports THAT a draft was held
+> for the owner, never WHY. Closing that needs a capture change and is
+> deliberately out of scope. Terms: `CONTEXT.md` → **Refusal**, **Refusal
+> category**, **Refusal source**, **Restraint tab**.
+
+| File | What it does |
+|------|--------------|
+| `vocabulary.ts` | The two-axis refusal vocabulary, pure — no DB, no clock. Axis A `REFUSAL_CATEGORIES`: the closed seven in precedence order, on the **Withhold reason** model (`WITHHOLD_REASONS` — `as const` array, union derived from it, `REFUSAL_CATEGORY_META` keyed `Record<ClosedEnum, Meta>` so a drifted enum is a compile error). Axis B `REFUSAL_SOURCES`: six lanes (`held_draft`, `auto_post_revoked`, `autonomy_suspended`, `ask_withheld`, `outreach_withheld`, `batch_halted`) — the map is PER-LANE because one code means different things in different lanes. 42 source codes: 25 to a category, 17 to explicit `null`. Traps: `null` is the recorded decision "not a refusal", NEVER an omission — the test uses `Object.hasOwn`, not truthiness; `refusalCategoryFor` THROWS `UnknownRefusalCodeError` rather than defaulting, because a default files an unmapped refusal under a plausible heading and the owner cannot tell the difference; `admin_halt`/`platform_halt` map to `autonomy_paused` on `batch_halted` but to `null` on `outreach_withheld` (`latchHalt` copies a batch halt through to every cancelled row, so counting both reports one halt again per recipient); `held_draft` has NO code axis, since `flagged_responses.reason` is moderation prose that may never leave the server. Four vocabularies are `satisfies Record<SourceEnum, RefusalCategory or null>`-guarded; `BATCH_HALT_REASONS` and `OUTREACH_WITHHELD_REASONS` have no TypeScript owner — their authority is the migration 111 CHECK, so a test parses `supabase/migrations/111_platform_halt_reason.sql` instead. Also exports `RENDER_WITHHOLD_REASONS` for the reason-dependent S6 anchor. **Keep this file `import type`-only.** It is imported by `'use client'` components for `REFUSAL_CATEGORY_META`, and a value import whose graph reaches `node:crypto` — as `@/lib/approval/audit` does — drags it into the browser bundle. No ESLint rule catches that pair; invariant #1's list does not cover it. This is why the S3 audit-action key space lives in `read.ts` and not here. |
+| `read.ts` | The one owner-safe read: `getRefusalLog(sql, scope, window)` — same three-parameter shape as `getAgentTeamSummary`, same `DashboardReadScope` discriminant (renamed from `AgentTeamScope`, so both owner reads share one scope vocabulary), same `dashboardWindowParams` handling. ONE statement: `SELECT … FROM ( <arm> UNION ALL <arm> … ) AS refusal_entries ORDER BY occurred_at DESC, entry_id DESC` — one wrapper, one sort, one five-column set (`entry_id, source, code, occurred_at, review_id`), issued sequentially and never through a `Promise` combinator. Adding an arm: alias its root `r`, spell `r.account_id = ${scope.accountId}` in the arm, add `AND ${inLocation}` as its OWN conjunct (omit for account-wide S4), project the five columns. Traps: `ci:tenant-scope` substitutes one `$N` per `${}` in the raw template text, so a fragment placed straight after the account predicate reads as `r.account_id = $1 $2` and the arm is reported UNSCOPED — hence the standalone `AND ${inLocation}` / `TRUE` conjunct; a backtick inside a SQL comment TERMINATES the tagged template (cost a cross-task `TS1005`); because the arms share one statement, a structural assertion over the combined SQL cannot prove any single arm is scoped, so every arm carries its own behavioural foreign-account/foreign-location negative-row test; the window needs an UPPER bound the `getAgentTeamSummary` precedent lacks. Never selects `flagged_responses.reason` — not projected, not filtered on, not logged. Also the **server-only home of the S3 audit-action key space**: the five `AUDIT_ACTION_*` value imports, the reason→action table, `deriveActionCategories` and `ConflictingRefusalActionError`. That lives here rather than in `vocabulary.ts` because `@/lib/approval/audit` reaches `node:crypto` and the vocabulary is client-imported. The DB stores five audit ACTIONS where `AutoPolicyDowngradeReason` has six members (`DOWNGRADE_MARKERS` collapses `judge_disabled` and `judge_unhealthy`), so the action map is DERIVED from the reason map rather than written twice, and a collapse whose two reasons carry different categories throws. Consequently `refusalCategoryFor` alone cannot resolve an action without a cycle: `resolveRefusalCategory` (exported here) is base-lookup plus the one S3 step, delegating unchanged for every other lane. Injected `Sql` (invariant #15) — the invariant test is a whole-file substring scan, so even prose naming `@/lib/db/server` fails it. |
+
 ### `reviews/`
 
 Everything about fetching and normalising Google reviews.
diff --git a/src/app/(authenticated)/dashboard/analytics/restraint/page.tsx b/src/app/(authenticated)/dashboard/analytics/restraint/page.tsx
