## P1 — The projection cannot support the claimed durable record

**Decision challenged:** Re-derive history from six operational tables on every read instead of recording an immutable refusal event.

**Concrete failure:** After 180 days, `ArchivalPoller` deletes terminal `agent_runs` ([ArchivalPoller.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/worker/background/pollers/ArchivalPoller.ts:222)). The outreach FK then sets `agent_run_id` to `NULL` ([091_send_mode_spine_lifecycle.sql](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/supabase/migrations/091_send_mode_spine_lifecycle.sql:256)). Consequently, every dispatch-time S6 refusal—not only the acknowledged best-effort attach failure—switches from immutable `run.started_at` to mutable `outreach.updated_at` ([read.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/src/lib/refusals/read.ts:587)). A later update can therefore rewrite its historical date or move it between windows once archival runs. ADR 0023 understates this as affecting only rows that originally lacked a run.

The same model allows current vocabulary changes to recategorize old decisions and makes any incompatible change to one source schema fail the entire log. Destructive offboarding deliberately deletes S1/S5/S6/S7 dependencies and responses, runs, and reviews, while scrubbing S4’s trigger payload ([purge-stages.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:294), [purge-stages.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:369), [purge-stages.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/worker/jobs/account-offboarding/purge-stages.ts:639)). That purge may be correct privacy behavior, but it proves this is not an independently durable record.

Finally, the owner cannot retrieve two years anyway: the largest selectable window is 90 days ([types.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/src/lib/kpi/types.ts:12)). The ADR’s present-tense “two-year record” claim is therefore unsupported both in storage semantics and UI access.

**Alternative:** Add an append-only `refusal_events` ledger written transactionally at decision time, containing immutable `occurred_at`, tenant/location scope, lane, source code, safe category/version, and an optional safe subject reference. Define two-year retention explicitly, make offboarding purge it according to policy, and add a two-year/paginated read. Operational tables can remain optional drill-down sources, never historical authority.

## P1 — The highest-volume refusal is absent, making the zero-result claim false

**Decision challenged:** Ship only the six readily queryable sources while treating `AutoApprovalBlockReason` as an explanation gap.

**Concrete failure:** This is not merely “THAT, but not WHY.” A normal auto-approval rejection writes only `policy_gate: { decision: 'held_for_owner' }` ([persist.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/worker/jobs/response-generation/persist.ts:195)); its 22 detailed reasons are log-only. Neither `agent_run_steps` nor the generic held-for-owner response is an arm of the refusal query. S1 is a different event: a high-severity moderation hold that never reaches the policy gate.

An account can therefore have drafts held because of daily-cap exhaustion, rating thresholds, entitlement, stale content, or another auto-approval prerequisite while `getRefusalLog` returns zero. The screen then asserts: “We did not hold anything back… Everything we were asked to do, we did” ([RestraintReport.tsx](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/src/components/dashboard/restraint/RestraintReport.tsx:146)). That directly contradicts “What we chose not to do for you, and why.”

**Alternative:** Record the auto-approval refusal and its safe mapped causes transactionally with the response decision. I would not ship an exhaustive title, total, or zero-result assertion until that lane is captured. If capture must wait, rename the surface to explicitly describe a partial set of safety interventions and make zero mean “none among the decisions currently tracked.”

## P2 — The source axis is discarded before it can explain “why”

**Decision challenged:** Maintain a precise per-lane source mapping, then expose only broad category copy and category-level destinations.

**Concrete failure:** `autonomy_paused` combines runtime disablement, lost Autopilot entitlement, policy suspension, Judge/Governor suspension, and admin/platform halts, but every entry renders the same sentence and links to auto-approval settings ([vocabulary.ts](/Users/norbertesekiel/Developer/startups/RepWorth-worktrees/feat-refusal-log/src/lib/refusals/vocabulary.ts:420)). That destination is not an honest remedy for lost entitlement or a platform halt. Similar collapsing means the UI explains the class of outcome, not why the individual decision happened.

The per-lane model itself is justified: `admin_halt` and `platform_halt` genuinely require `(lane, code)` semantics to prevent double-counting. A global map plus exclusions would recreate that composite key less clearly. The problem is that the useful distinction never reaches owner presentation.

**Alternative:** Keep the two-axis vocabulary, but attach an owner-safe per-source cause label and action target to each event. Use categories for aggregation; use the sanitized lane/code mapping for the individual explanation and relevant next step.

## LOW-CONFIDENCE APPENDIX

- I would not replace the `UNION ALL` with six TypeScript reads. A single snapshot, one sort, and all-or-nothing failure are appropriate for a total that claims completeness; returning five successful lanes after one fails would be more misleading. The 637-line statement is cumbersome, but adding a seventh arm is not materially harder than adding a seventh read plus merge logic. The durable-ledger issue is the real seam problem.
- Destructive offboarding alone is not a reason to retain refusal history after deletion: a new ledger should participate in the same privacy lifecycle. It matters here because the ADR calls the current projection durable without defining that lifecycle boundary.

VERDICT: REVISE
